WEBVTT

00:00.658 --> 00:04.291
Yep, he's ready. I'm ready. Okay. All right,

00:04.596 --> 00:08.213
it is 4:01, Tuesday the 18th, and we welcome

00:08.713 --> 00:10.858
you here as we start our work session.

00:12.489 --> 00:15.088
Thanks for coming out, and for all those that are joining online and virtually,

00:15.104 --> 00:19.135
we appreciate you helping us out and joining the process. So we'll

00:19.635 --> 00:22.061
start with the first item,

00:23.218 --> 00:27.136
discussion on the Pony Express Business Park. And I believe it's

00:27.636 --> 00:28.726
Steven, and he is ready.

00:42.451 --> 00:46.540
All right. So we've actually— we've been working on this project for several months

00:47.706 --> 00:51.341
with the applicant. As always,

00:51.357 --> 00:54.474
some background. A few months ago, this did come to the Planning Commission.

00:55.443 --> 00:58.620
There was a variance tied to these properties that was ultimately denied

00:59.598 --> 01:04.620
based on just not meeting the criteria there for that one. So as

01:04.732 --> 01:07.765
a workaround of that and trying to get their site to work, they're now proposing

01:07.813 --> 01:10.989
a development agreement. And before we made them go too far along in the process,

01:11.070 --> 01:14.198
they wanted to speak with city council members to see if that's something that they're

01:14.920 --> 01:18.883
willing to entertain and what specific aspects they need to focus on as

01:18.947 --> 01:22.688
they draft that agreement. So this

01:22.800 --> 01:25.829
is the site overlaid here.

01:27.471 --> 01:31.209
So it's 3— so these 2 lots here— I'll go back here.

01:31.371 --> 01:34.838
So it's these 2 lots in question. There is a plat amendment

01:35.338 --> 01:37.786
that they have right now to move this lot line up to about here.

01:41.300 --> 01:44.253
So this would be the site. It would be 3 buildings across the 2 lots.

01:48.257 --> 01:49.396
Here's a zoom in a little bit.

01:54.350 --> 01:57.606
As part of the application, they— and this is part of their variance application.

01:57.655 --> 01:59.348
I took this to help you see it.

02:01.607 --> 02:05.196
One of the main exceptions they're seeking is a setback exception from the

02:05.229 --> 02:08.786
wash, where we have now in our code where they have

02:09.286 --> 02:13.197
to have a 50-foot setback from the top bank of the wash or 75-foot

02:13.697 --> 02:18.436
from the center flow line, whichever one is greater. So they proposed— this

02:18.936 --> 02:22.433
one is light blue is where they said is the center flow of

02:22.626 --> 02:26.557
the wash, as well as the top bank of the wash is that outer shading

02:26.606 --> 02:30.050
of that blue. A few months ago

02:30.550 --> 02:32.453
when I went out there and looked at it, I mapped what I thought was

02:32.469 --> 02:36.372
the actual top bank of the wash. So I outlined that in blue here,

02:36.840 --> 02:40.190
and then the red lines are just their buffers that they made.

02:40.868 --> 02:44.002
So you can see there's a— depending on which flow

02:44.018 --> 02:47.249
or top of the bank you actually follow, those lines would actually change.

02:48.530 --> 02:51.446
Ultimately, it's our city engineer who determines what that top bank of the wash is

02:52.677 --> 02:55.285
and then ultimately what those setbacks would be.

02:59.190 --> 03:02.544
So these are the requests. The first one we mentioned

03:02.560 --> 03:06.126
is the ability to work within and construct buffers or

03:06.191 --> 03:09.630
buildings within those buffers. They do propose

03:11.354 --> 03:14.578
along here to have retaining walls to make their site work, which would go

03:15.078 --> 03:19.391
up to— I think they're around 10 or 11 feet

03:19.505 --> 03:22.277
in height. So that wouldn't be any staggering or tiers. It would just be a

03:22.777 --> 03:26.174
sheer wall. And then they also want exceptions to not have to

03:26.674 --> 03:29.875
construct the drive-through escape lanes. So again,

03:29.891 --> 03:33.664
there's the site. So I feel like they don't have enough room to To

03:34.164 --> 03:37.077
put those in, so that's really what they're asking for.

03:37.431 --> 03:40.827
And ultimately, they want to know: should they spend the

03:41.327 --> 03:44.871
money to design those walls if that's something that is going

03:45.371 --> 03:48.704
to be acceptable in this development agreement, or any other feedback you're willing to provide

03:49.204 --> 03:52.286
them so they don't they don't waste their time or staff

03:52.335 --> 03:53.960
time, and we can move forward with this.

03:57.655 --> 03:59.482
Are we able to chime in as the applicant?

04:02.919 --> 04:06.634
Yeah, I don't mind, uh, if there's some questions. Do you have some context

04:07.134 --> 04:09.929
you want to add? Yeah, I think there's just some added context.

04:10.640 --> 04:13.497
Um, you'll notice, uh,

04:13.804 --> 04:17.177
on the plans you were shown a different sheet. I don't know if

04:17.436 --> 04:21.930
we can go back to show that, but it shows a green border around

04:22.060 --> 04:25.606
this whole project. You still

04:26.106 --> 04:29.115
have that? No, not that one.

04:29.628 --> 04:31.679
Actually, that one works. That one works. Go back to that one.

04:32.496 --> 04:35.171
Um, when we, when we first started this development,

04:36.021 --> 04:39.417
um, I think it's important to note that we actually gave up

04:39.561 --> 04:42.846
2 acres of the, the project. It's a pretty small project,

04:42.862 --> 04:46.339
so 2 acres was a lot. Um, part of that was along

04:46.451 --> 04:49.864
the main road, as you see there, that setback to the yellow line.

04:50.681 --> 04:54.832
Um, but then also part of that was to mitigate Or

04:54.928 --> 04:57.528
to provide the city room,

04:58.459 --> 05:02.406
um, and a buffer to mitigate any concerns with the

05:02.906 --> 05:06.434
Tickville Wash. As a part of that giving or

05:06.934 --> 05:10.590
that taking, um, you know, that left us much smaller lots

05:11.090 --> 05:14.907
here, but workable lots. Uh, but that also, you know, as we

05:15.407 --> 05:18.774
come back now with our plan, um, it,

05:19.240 --> 05:22.898
it feels a little bit like, you know, They need more and more

05:23.398 --> 05:25.757
and more. So we took this to Planning Commission. I think an important thing to

05:25.790 --> 05:28.985
note, Planning Commission was actually very supportive of working

05:29.485 --> 05:34.854
with us, knowing the history of this

05:35.032 --> 05:39.488
giving and the specific giving to mitigate any

05:39.504 --> 05:43.039
concerns with the Tickville Wash. So I think that's an important thing

05:43.136 --> 05:46.134
to note. The reason, if I'm not mistaken,

05:46.150 --> 05:50.024
that it was denied is because within the code there wasn't an option necessary

05:50.138 --> 05:53.229
to accept accept them under the— under how they were presented.

05:53.293 --> 05:56.876
So they— so, so that we then moved

05:56.908 --> 06:00.186
to development agreement, which was the, the secondary

06:00.686 --> 06:03.174
option to do it. But I just want to highlight, we've not felt as we've

06:03.674 --> 06:07.479
come, and we've not felt in our continued partnership with Eagle Mountain,

06:07.736 --> 06:10.821
in addition to us giving up land, that there hasn't been an appetite to work

06:10.869 --> 06:14.789
with us. In fact, it's been stated, um, by Planning Commission

06:15.062 --> 06:17.617
and really everyone we've talked to that, hey, we want to make this we want

06:18.117 --> 06:20.667
to work with you. The challenge we have now, now that our lot lines have

06:21.167 --> 06:24.136
been pushed back, now that we have given already land so that, so the city

06:24.216 --> 06:27.909
can mitigate concerns with the wash, there's not a lot

06:28.409 --> 06:32.019
we can do with the lots. They're really small now in a retail sense.

06:32.068 --> 06:35.536
They're really small. So we're just trying to make it work and make it work

06:36.036 --> 06:39.935
in a way that it brings a great building to, you know, some great

06:40.435 --> 06:43.699
commercial buildings to the city and And whatnot.

06:43.747 --> 06:47.289
So those were the 2, call them variances or items in the development agreement we're

06:47.789 --> 06:49.790
hoping for City Council support.

06:54.539 --> 06:57.904
Okay, and I just had one question actually as a point of

06:58.404 --> 07:01.541
reference for Vince. Is it even— I don't know

07:02.041 --> 07:05.600
because of the natural aspect next to the park that we try to preserve there,

07:05.665 --> 07:09.629
but could this even be piped and what size and Or

07:09.645 --> 07:13.436
is that just not even— it could be piped.

07:13.920 --> 07:17.518
If you've seen the culvert underneath the roadway, it would

07:17.534 --> 07:20.503
have to be that same size. And so I would think it would be pretty

07:20.600 --> 07:24.314
cost prohibitive to do that. Well, that's what I'm looking for. I don't know

07:24.814 --> 07:26.622
there's an appetite for that. I just wanted it to be out on the table

07:26.671 --> 07:30.439
that if it was, what that would be, so the council would know

07:32.336 --> 07:35.455
if they could mitigate that flow line. It would be expensive.

07:35.616 --> 07:39.168
It's possible, but expensive. And it would have to be a 60 plus.

07:39.248 --> 07:42.995
Is it bigger than 60? It's probably a square. I think it is a square,

07:43.544 --> 07:45.840
and it's yeah, it's bigger than 60 for sure.

07:47.105 --> 07:49.926
Okay, I just throwing out context for that.

07:51.628 --> 07:55.308
We've heard the presentation. Is there any questions for the applicant or for Stephen?

07:55.518 --> 07:58.906
You guys. Oh, sorry. This is

07:58.923 --> 08:02.458
probably Stephen or Marcus. Do we

08:02.958 --> 08:05.049
know with a two-acre strip the frontage?

08:06.199 --> 08:10.129
Fronting on Pony Express, if that was compensated or

08:10.177 --> 08:11.704
if it was just dedicated to the city?

08:15.895 --> 08:19.570
I don't know the answer to that question. I'd have

08:20.070 --> 08:25.307
to— yeah, I didn't look at the exact— I

08:25.404 --> 08:29.284
can speak to that. Those were given to the city. There was no compensation

08:29.301 --> 08:30.116
for the 2 acres.

08:33.549 --> 08:37.512
It was a 6-acre site and we ended up with 4 acres. Can we

08:38.012 --> 08:42.368
switch to the overhead that shows where the building

08:42.549 --> 08:43.685
would be for the site plan?

08:47.712 --> 08:48.857
That one, or do you want a different one?

08:51.907 --> 08:55.025
Is there one that's see-through that just has the outlines of the buildings?

08:57.707 --> 08:58.914
No, I wasn't that nice.

09:01.518 --> 09:05.464
I, I'm just going to be really frank. I think the thing for me that's

09:05.964 --> 09:09.862
concerning isn't about the city mitigating the wash.

09:10.024 --> 09:13.737
It's about whether or not that terrain is suitable

09:13.802 --> 09:15.852
for building a commercial entity on.

09:16.772 --> 09:20.218
And I've driven by that wash

09:20.984 --> 09:24.677
multiple times a day for almost 2 decades, and I know

09:24.741 --> 09:28.255
that there's changes and you can walk by it

09:28.304 --> 09:31.769
now and see where there's some erosion and different things that

09:32.269 --> 09:35.733
are taking place. So I'm a little hesitant

09:36.233 --> 09:39.536
to say go for it, 'cause I feel like in a lot of ways we'd

09:39.601 --> 09:43.009
be like the city of Draper letting a home developer

09:43.074 --> 09:46.800
build on a hill and then having the homes fall into a pit.

09:47.268 --> 09:50.447
I just, I'm concerned about how close it is to the wash.

09:52.367 --> 09:56.771
And just because we can do it doesn't mean we should. So to

09:56.804 --> 09:57.953
quote Jurassic Park,

10:00.219 --> 10:03.738
I want these businesses to be able to grow here.

10:04.206 --> 10:08.065
I'm just wondering if maybe Vince,

10:08.130 --> 10:11.947
you could speak to what will happen

10:12.011 --> 10:16.180
there because even the wall or fence that's behind

10:16.680 --> 10:18.579
Cascade Collision and then Burt Brothers.

10:20.282 --> 10:23.981
I just— it's right there and it always

10:24.174 --> 10:27.906
feels like it's not in a safe place. Can you talk

10:27.970 --> 10:31.727
about that? Yeah, it's a natural channel and the banks do

10:32.227 --> 10:35.649
shift over time. And as we've

10:35.665 --> 10:38.973
looked at this in different reviews that we've done, I have expressed

10:39.473 --> 10:42.587
concern. Even if there's a retaining wall there, what are they doing

10:42.700 --> 10:46.223
to protect the base of that retaining wall? Because it is going to be in

10:46.273 --> 10:49.748
a flow line. And that's

10:50.248 --> 10:52.905
nothing new. Those concerns have been expressed in the past.

10:52.969 --> 10:56.077
But it can be done engineering-wise.

10:57.076 --> 11:00.554
And I've expressed that to the applicants that yes, we can do

11:01.054 --> 11:04.338
this. We have to do it the right way. But yeah, those banks do shift

11:04.838 --> 11:08.791
and they will have to be protected. Follow-up. I think

11:08.855 --> 11:12.015
we're becoming more astute and savvy when

11:12.515 --> 11:16.647
it comes to grade and what's going on on a site plan.

11:18.109 --> 11:21.259
I think we've often looked at the site plan and said,

11:21.339 --> 11:24.608
yeah, that fits there. But is there anything

11:24.656 --> 11:27.865
about the grade or the slope of this particular place

11:27.979 --> 11:30.183
that is going to need to be mitigated?

11:33.009 --> 11:37.275
If you're referring to like the 25% maximum,

11:37.894 --> 11:41.186
I think The grade into the wash itself from

11:41.218 --> 11:43.971
the top of the bank is definitely steeper than that.

11:44.341 --> 11:47.223
In fact, there's some sheer, very sheer areas,

11:47.883 --> 11:50.975
and I think that's why they were going to the retaining wall idea.

11:51.218 --> 11:53.552
That would be the only real way to do that.

11:54.510 --> 11:56.617
Can you switch to the diagram that shows,

11:58.756 --> 12:01.683
like, the top of the— the 2 different comparisons? So,

12:01.700 --> 12:05.276
Steven, will you tell us again what the red lines mean?

12:08.018 --> 12:12.359
So the 2 red lines are the buffers that come off of this really light

12:12.408 --> 12:16.133
blue line. So the

12:16.633 --> 12:19.997
first, the inside one is 50 feet from the flow, or 75, no, 50 feet

12:20.497 --> 12:23.651
from the top bank, and then the one outside

12:24.151 --> 12:28.823
of that is 75 feet from the flow line. And the

12:28.904 --> 12:32.009
blue lines represent what, where the top of the bank is? The blue line is

12:32.106 --> 12:35.529
when I went out and walked the wash. Well, I didn't. I wasn't down in

12:36.029 --> 12:39.410
the wash. I was on their side, and that's what I estimate is the

12:39.910 --> 12:40.542
actual top bank of the wash.

12:45.532 --> 12:49.109
Okay, thank you. Could I, as the applicant, provide a little more

12:49.609 --> 12:49.918
context as well?

12:52.250 --> 12:53.414
Sure.

12:55.534 --> 12:59.369
So we we've heard Eagle Mountains.

12:59.962 --> 13:03.379
Concern with the wash. And we too don't want— these,

13:03.491 --> 13:07.148
these are buildings actually, we don't, um, there's no turn and burn

13:07.244 --> 13:10.790
on these. We as a builder, we actually keep most of the assets

13:11.290 --> 13:14.928
we build. So we're going to be the owners of these buildings. Um, not as

13:14.960 --> 13:17.735
though we'd want to build something for a client and then run away and watch

13:17.767 --> 13:21.874
their building, you know, have some challenges with any sort of elevations

13:22.374 --> 13:25.948
or washes. But we're particularly interested in making sure these last the test, you know,

13:26.029 --> 13:29.737
stand the test of time. And so we have already contracted with the engineers.

13:29.786 --> 13:33.415
They've provided us a letter which I can send over to you guys that

13:33.915 --> 13:37.720
speaks to not only in the design of

13:37.752 --> 13:41.366
the wall, you know, making sure the grades and the soils

13:41.866 --> 13:45.349
are all sure, sure, you know, sure soils to

13:45.365 --> 13:49.075
provide a sure foundation, but also that the wall is built in

13:49.575 --> 13:52.897
a way that can withstand any changes

13:52.929 --> 13:56.327
in the bank. So, so that is all being

13:56.359 --> 13:59.740
thought through. We, we hear the city— actually, the city's concerns

13:59.757 --> 14:03.287
on that are our concerns. We, we want to make sure this withstands the

14:03.787 --> 14:06.819
test of time, and we want to make sure it's engineered in the right way,

14:06.900 --> 14:09.548
which, which frankly,

14:10.050 --> 14:13.466
um, should, should be better than

14:13.483 --> 14:15.992
a moving bank in a lot of ways. An unsecured bank,

14:16.138 --> 14:19.669
unsecured hills. Um, You know,

14:19.928 --> 14:23.698
soils, you know, you will have that side of the bank that's really shored

14:24.198 --> 14:27.643
up for the future as well. Really? Is it okay if I

14:27.676 --> 14:31.200
ask another question, Mayor? Okay, so Stephen,

14:31.571 --> 14:35.370
there's the northernmost building on this diagram right now. Is that

14:35.870 --> 14:39.609
set back as far as it is because of commercial setback requirements, or is that

14:39.882 --> 14:41.492
the applicant's preference?

14:43.569 --> 14:46.305
I can't speak to their preference, but yeah, it would meet the setbacks for that.

14:47.013 --> 14:50.674
For the zone, I guess for me, I'd much rather see that

14:50.786 --> 14:54.046
get closer to the edge of that front property line as

14:54.094 --> 14:57.658
opposed to almost an entire building

14:58.317 --> 15:01.929
in the no-build zone to the

15:02.429 --> 15:06.136
south of that. And I'm just wondering if there's any appetite to make

15:06.636 --> 15:09.491
adjustments there. I just feel like it's so close to the wash.

15:10.535 --> 15:14.067
Mayor, can I just jump in? I'll just— I'll share a 30-second opinion. I mean,

15:14.132 --> 15:17.824
I think that the code Surrounding the wash and the setback

15:18.324 --> 15:22.142
requirements is there for a reason. I, for one, am going to really struggle to

15:22.191 --> 15:25.972
want to enter into a development agreement to remove those standards.

15:27.618 --> 15:31.200
In particular, in a space that is obviously impacted by

15:31.652 --> 15:35.177
the wash. I mean, there's a city park directly south

15:35.209 --> 15:38.455
of this, and that's an integral part of the

15:38.955 --> 15:42.432
park where we've got some signage and some trails that are already

15:43.173 --> 15:46.674
functioning in that area. So For me, it's unfortunate.

15:46.738 --> 15:49.856
It's a tough piece of property to develop. My hat goes off to

15:50.356 --> 15:53.520
you as a developer in trying to figure this out. But for me,

15:53.810 --> 15:57.426
I'm going to err on the side of maintaining

15:57.459 --> 15:59.781
those code standards on this piece of property.

16:07.290 --> 16:10.020
And I think I feel the same as well as Councilmember Wright.

16:14.668 --> 16:18.289
Yeah, I would echo that. We have other areas in our city where this wasn't

16:18.433 --> 16:22.007
really followed, and the embankments have shifted, and homeowners have had

16:22.135 --> 16:25.549
foundations crack. In fact, we had a resident that a couple

16:26.049 --> 16:29.010
months ago I went out to, to look at that, and it's just created issues.

16:29.571 --> 16:33.256
And, um, I, I think I'm probably going to

16:33.756 --> 16:37.118
fall within the lines of the code that, that exists. And I would say

16:37.618 --> 16:41.412
not to mention our area, but other areas where they didn't follow this and

16:41.912 --> 16:45.258
houses get swept away. But I just wanted maybe a quick yes or no.

16:45.979 --> 16:49.312
If the reason I asked that question before, if there was say gabion baskets or

16:49.760 --> 16:52.997
piping, would that change anything? It's— I don't

16:53.497 --> 16:56.330
know that it's even possible. I was just curious. Probably for me, the answer would

16:56.830 --> 16:59.774
be no. I'm just— they're looking for direction. I just want to make sure that

17:00.274 --> 17:03.940
we've exhausted. I could easily give on the drive-through

17:04.440 --> 17:07.694
escape lane. That part, I'm not married

17:08.194 --> 17:08.753
to that aspect.

17:12.110 --> 17:15.499
Didn't change anybody on— OK. I'll take that as it does. Just so that the

17:15.516 --> 17:18.357
applicant can know that they don't come back and say, well, what if we do

17:18.857 --> 17:22.248
baskets, or what if we do— I just want to make sure that we're

17:22.748 --> 17:26.043
sticking to the code on this one. Is that— any other questions?

17:26.851 --> 17:29.580
OK. Thanks, Stephen. Appreciate it.

17:31.731 --> 17:35.534
The next one, uh, I'm actually excited

17:36.034 --> 17:38.983
to hear about this as an avid shooter myself and,

17:40.611 --> 17:44.274
uh, working with, with our— it went from the interns

17:45.014 --> 17:49.770
to Ethan and Addie to Ethan and

17:49.914 --> 17:53.549
Addie as individuals. And so I loved watching them earn their own

17:53.581 --> 17:56.895
names and not just be interns. I understand Ethan's online.

17:57.886 --> 18:00.964
I hope. Yes, yes, that's right. Okay,

18:01.654 --> 18:04.042
uh, hang on. We— oh, there's— there it is.

18:04.491 --> 18:07.329
Okay, go ahead. Okay, thank you very much,

18:07.665 --> 18:11.609
Mayor and Council. I wish that I could be there in person today

18:12.109 --> 18:15.424
to be able to thank you for the intern experience that I've had.

18:16.178 --> 18:20.426
Unfortunately, I had a surprise trip to the ER

18:20.926 --> 18:24.513
yesterday morning for some eye problems, but I've been able to

18:25.939 --> 18:29.400
confine myself to a dark room and— but I'm

18:29.900 --> 18:33.486
prepared, ready to present. So thank you for bearing with me through this.

18:34.111 --> 18:37.956
And thank you for a wonderful summer,

18:37.973 --> 18:41.321
a great internship experience. I feel like I've been able

18:41.821 --> 18:45.343
to see a lot of unique things happen just in the span of a couple

18:45.843 --> 18:49.573
months, which will provide some great context and experience

18:50.073 --> 18:53.781
for the rest of my program. At BYU and hopefully future

18:54.281 --> 18:57.617
jobs down the line, wherever that may be. So thank you for,

18:57.989 --> 18:59.462
for making this a great summer for me.

19:01.440 --> 19:03.753
So yes, this is about a month or 2 ago.

19:04.287 --> 19:08.153
Mayor came to us, and I think there were other couple employees

19:08.428 --> 19:11.708
that had similar experiences of residents who wanted to

19:11.724 --> 19:15.301
practice archery, but they weren't really sure where or how or if

19:15.801 --> 19:18.794
it was legal at all. And well, it really it wasn't.

19:19.035 --> 19:22.586
People couldn't shoot in their backyards, and so

19:23.086 --> 19:26.620
the goal was to provide a way for archery ranges to be

19:26.668 --> 19:30.524
housed in Eagle Mountain. And technically,

19:30.605 --> 19:34.638
you could say there there was a way, but it

19:35.138 --> 19:38.543
wasn't very fleshed out or regulated,

19:39.298 --> 19:42.928
especially to the same extent that. an outdoor

19:43.428 --> 19:47.030
shooting range was. So for context, this is all

19:47.078 --> 19:50.123
that there really is in the code right now, or this is all that there

19:50.623 --> 19:55.074
is regarding archery, that residents

19:55.574 --> 19:59.000
couldn't discharge a bow more than 10 pounds of pull weight,

19:59.016 --> 20:02.718
which is really nothing. That's practically a toy bow with

20:03.218 --> 20:06.531
a suction cup on the end of it. And it

20:07.031 --> 20:10.862
had to go through Planning Commission review for any applicants. But other than that,

20:10.943 --> 20:14.510
there, there were not very many standards or guidelines

20:14.558 --> 20:18.559
for applicants to consider when they wanted to propose

20:18.591 --> 20:20.857
to build an archery range within the city.

20:21.805 --> 20:25.404
So the goal was to provide a more robust framework for that.

20:26.448 --> 20:30.771
And the best way that I found to do that would be to combine

20:30.867 --> 20:34.997
the outdoor shooting range overlay zone with the archery range.

20:35.703 --> 20:39.494
With an archery range zone. So those would become one zone because

20:39.994 --> 20:44.023
many of the same standards that outdoor shooting ranges have,

20:44.617 --> 20:47.781
archery ranges could follow many of those as well.

20:48.793 --> 20:51.893
So this amendment would add archery range to that overlay zone,

20:52.567 --> 20:55.780
and then that would be able to provide for all the operational

20:56.280 --> 21:00.534
and safety standards. And it would also require

21:00.598 --> 21:04.494
that the applicant obtained the land use authority approval

21:05.727 --> 21:10.294
as part of the application process. So some

21:10.794 --> 21:14.059
of the key changes or the key things that I've added that

21:14.559 --> 21:17.311
are different for archery ranges than for shooting ranges.

21:18.449 --> 21:21.525
One would be to refer the applicant to

21:22.326 --> 21:25.723
safety standards. The national or

21:25.835 --> 21:29.568
the sort of governing body on archery participant safety is

21:29.584 --> 21:33.271
USA Archery. So that would require the applicant

21:33.771 --> 21:38.098
to consider their safety guidelines. It also provides a

21:38.179 --> 21:41.466
line regarding backstop and projectile containment,

21:41.851 --> 21:45.363
because that's also very separate, very different from shooting ranges.

21:45.491 --> 21:48.811
And then I've also provided different

21:48.859 --> 21:52.580
definitions for bows and crossbows, because in

21:52.772 --> 21:56.829
certain scenarios, those should be treated very differently, especially considering

21:56.861 --> 22:00.550
that crossbows have more of a trigger mechanism similar to

22:01.050 --> 22:05.364
firearms. And bows can certainly be scaled

22:05.749 --> 22:07.739
differently. They have different pull weights.

22:09.136 --> 22:12.458
Some are for children and some are for adults. So I've

22:12.490 --> 22:16.535
also outlined different

22:16.663 --> 22:20.467
age groups should have different standards. So the age

22:20.967 --> 22:24.961
of 8 is where in the outdoor shooting range overlay

22:25.461 --> 22:29.149
zone children are not allowed to use firearms

22:29.198 --> 22:33.336
if they're under the age of 8, but bow safety

22:33.352 --> 22:36.749
is a little bit separate. So I have allowed for children under

22:37.249 --> 22:41.386
the age of 8 to use bows, as long as within USA Archery guidelines.

22:42.867 --> 22:47.054
And then finally, there within the special use

22:48.719 --> 22:52.989
chapter within the code, there is indoor shooting range standards.

22:54.005 --> 22:57.519
But I have mirrored all of the same safety standards from the outdoor range

22:57.616 --> 23:01.565
to the indoor range, so those would all look similar.

23:03.787 --> 23:07.313
And that is essentially all the changes. It's mainly

23:07.409 --> 23:10.533
just an addition of archery

23:10.871 --> 23:14.227
into the outdoor overlay zone, outdoor shooting range overlay

23:14.727 --> 23:18.462
zone. I'm curious to hear if there are

23:18.962 --> 23:22.742
any other safety standards you would like to see considered or

23:22.839 --> 23:26.383
potentially added to this code as we move forward.

23:27.467 --> 23:30.731
So, thank you. And I'm willing

23:30.763 --> 23:34.231
to pull up— I can have the, uh, the code

23:34.731 --> 23:38.428
as I, as I've edited it pulled up, um, if you have questions.

23:42.860 --> 23:46.719
Thank you for putting this together. As a kid that grew up on a on

23:47.219 --> 23:50.180
an archery team. It's fun to see something like this come forward.

23:50.940 --> 23:54.757
My question is regarding the age of 8. That seems

23:55.257 --> 23:58.189
a little young. I, I would probably consider maybe 12 or 13.

23:59.258 --> 24:03.242
Um, I'm curious where— how

24:03.307 --> 24:07.131
you came to the age 8 to— yeah,

24:07.860 --> 24:11.166
I might raise that up a little bit. Does that make sense? Of course.

24:11.247 --> 24:13.920
The question? Yeah. I can absolutely consider that. Yeah,

24:14.497 --> 24:17.593
as I looked through the USA Archery guidelines,

24:18.572 --> 24:22.214
um, it had all of these amazing stats

24:22.714 --> 24:26.288
about how archery is one of the most safe recreational

24:26.788 --> 24:31.037
activities. And oftentimes there was sort

24:31.537 --> 24:34.486
of like an 8-year-old threshold where, um,

24:35.513 --> 24:40.694
as long as there is like adult, constant adult

24:41.194 --> 24:44.774
supervision with that 8-year-old. It can be practiced, but, you know, that's all I

24:44.823 --> 24:47.167
went off of. That can absolutely be changed and raised.

24:48.194 --> 24:51.229
I, I think, and it's been a minute, I know I only looked 35,

24:51.245 --> 24:54.697
but I haven't taken hunter safety. I think you can hunt at 12. You can

24:55.197 --> 24:58.567
hunt at 12, that's right. So, so 13 would be— I mean,

24:58.631 --> 25:01.602
you could have a license. I think that'd be too high. Yeah.

25:02.019 --> 25:06.307
So I

25:06.807 --> 25:10.738
think giving them an opportunity to experience that. I mean, these— it's

25:11.238 --> 25:14.538
in a controlled environment. They're going to have a range boss there, a parent.

25:15.361 --> 25:18.412
You're not going to drop an 8-year-old off to go shoot. You know, they'll be

25:18.912 --> 25:22.285
with somebody. So I think I'm less concerned about that in a controlled environment.

25:22.932 --> 25:26.414
Maybe if they were just out outside of that, that might be a different story.

25:28.280 --> 25:32.136
Another thing too, their insurance or liability insurance may end up

25:32.636 --> 25:37.210
naturally limiting age. So I'm inclined to let it be that

25:37.274 --> 25:41.413
8-year-old mark and kind of let the market forces kind

25:41.913 --> 25:45.458
of control it a little bit. So I would just recommend that we

25:45.587 --> 25:49.516
add on that portion that talks about the children under the age

25:49.548 --> 25:53.158
of 8 may be permitted to handle a bow with the supervision of a responsible

25:53.658 --> 25:57.498
adult, or, and in accordance with the USA Archery— I think it should say guidelines

25:57.595 --> 25:58.789
so that we know what that's referencing.

26:00.546 --> 26:05.175
Okay. But I have no issues with an 8-year-old. Shoot, I was doing crazier

26:05.675 --> 26:06.534
stuff than that at 8. Okay,

26:12.777 --> 26:16.268
I know your time is limited, so you, uh, you're going back to school.

26:16.317 --> 26:20.115
We appreciate you and all that you've done for our city, so hopefully

26:20.615 --> 26:23.735
we see you down the road. Absolutely. Thank you so much.

26:23.832 --> 26:26.574
Thank you all, Mayor and Council. Thank you. Yes, thank you.

26:28.293 --> 26:31.474
All right, another great, uh, presentation, and this one I'm

26:31.490 --> 26:33.916
really excited about for the council because, uh,

26:35.121 --> 26:38.692
this is something we've kind of been looking for. And as we brainstorm this idea

26:38.902 --> 26:42.538
of, uh, trying to kind of compare our staffing needs with,

26:42.716 --> 26:45.215
with our workload, uh,

26:46.327 --> 26:50.020
Addie and I sat down and with Natalie and others and, and tried

26:50.520 --> 26:53.053
to come up with a metric. So the idea here is if we're missing something,

26:53.281 --> 26:57.138
the we're not done. Give feedback to what else we

26:57.638 --> 27:01.032
can use for the, uh,

27:01.339 --> 27:05.221
what would help us make good decisions in the future. So good

27:05.237 --> 27:08.667
feedback is what we're looking for. And thank you very much

27:08.780 --> 27:09.880
for your time here as well.

27:13.344 --> 27:16.982
Hello, Mayor and Council. Thank you for allowing me to present today. I appreciate the

27:17.015 --> 27:20.402
opportunity. A little bit about me. My name

27:20.578 --> 27:24.205
is Addison Cragin. I'm an intern for Eagle Mountain City right now, and I'm

27:24.705 --> 27:28.747
studying at BYU in the MPA program. And I'm from Texas originally,

27:28.843 --> 27:32.021
but I've been in Utah for the last 6 years and have loved it.

27:33.192 --> 27:37.300
All right, today I'll be presenting about a plan for an analysis that looks

27:37.349 --> 27:40.542
at the staffing load— or staffing level versus the

27:40.574 --> 27:43.270
workload in cities close by to Eagle Mountain City.

27:44.747 --> 27:48.219
And this will just help us to see where Eagle Mountain stands compared to other

27:48.719 --> 27:52.521
cities that have different growth rates, population sizes, and other

27:53.021 --> 27:56.476
things like that. And we haven't

27:56.541 --> 27:59.979
started gathering data yet for this, and so we wanted to just present to you

28:00.011 --> 28:03.577
guys to get feedback so that we're making sure that it aligns with your

28:03.690 --> 28:05.923
goals and what you guys want out of this study.

28:07.633 --> 28:11.051
And I have a short presentation, and I will just have you guys leave

28:11.551 --> 28:14.378
the comments till the end. Just so we can show all the things that we

28:14.878 --> 28:15.130
have done so far.

28:18.090 --> 28:21.665
All right. So for this study, we are looking at cities that are similar

28:22.165 --> 28:26.062
to Eagle Mountain City, but also that have bigger population sizes,

28:26.480 --> 28:29.734
have different growth rates, and also just different projects,

28:29.750 --> 28:33.375
departments. And those cities are Herriman,

28:33.665 --> 28:37.187
Riverton, Saratoga Springs, Lehi, Orem,

28:37.401 --> 28:41.204
Provo, and Spanish Fork. These cities all have different situations

28:41.236 --> 28:45.247
and also maturity levels, which will help us to learn more about

28:45.344 --> 28:49.135
what we can learn from them, but also what we're already doing well here

28:49.168 --> 28:49.867
at Eagle Mountain City.

28:53.020 --> 28:56.123
All right, and we have a few areas of focus that we're looking at for

28:56.623 --> 28:59.695
this study, and those areas are demographics, parks and

29:00.195 --> 29:02.600
streets, trails and open space, water,

29:03.068 --> 29:06.280
recreation center, fire and rescue, and then also just staffing

29:06.600 --> 29:08.100
numbers overall at the city.

29:10.800 --> 29:14.286
All right. For this analysis, I will coordinate with the chosen

29:14.786 --> 29:18.448
cities that we've decided to investigate and send them a survey where we'll ask

29:18.948 --> 29:23.004
for the data points that we're going to use for this analysis. And here

29:23.132 --> 29:26.403
you can see the layout of the different areas and also

29:26.419 --> 29:29.310
of the different data points we're wanting to gather from those cities.

29:30.728 --> 29:33.238
And I'll just list off a few of them. It's not the whole list,

29:33.254 --> 29:36.503
but just the ones that are kind of the focus of what Mayor wanted us

29:36.600 --> 29:39.886
to look at. And those are how many acres

29:39.983 --> 29:43.848
of parks you have and also how many employees you have to maintain those parks.

29:44.653 --> 29:47.936
How many lane miles of road you maintain and also how many employees

29:47.968 --> 29:50.108
you have to help maintain those roads.

29:52.170 --> 29:55.730
And then does your fire department run on 3-hand crews or 4-hand

29:55.746 --> 29:59.516
crews? Those are a few of the ones that we're asking for and then

29:59.629 --> 30:03.178
also just a list of all the departments they have and, like, staffing numbers

30:03.678 --> 30:05.473
for each department, just to compare with us.

30:08.340 --> 30:11.665
All right. And so for this study, we hope that we can try and pinpoint

30:12.165 --> 30:15.906
areas that we're doing well, but also areas that we could improve

30:16.406 --> 30:20.109
on as we continue to grow in population size and also grow just

30:20.609 --> 30:24.354
in general with different departments and needs for the city. And so

30:24.370 --> 30:27.179
here's a few questions we hope to learn from this study.

30:27.602 --> 30:30.861
that we can help— hope you guys can apply as

30:30.893 --> 30:34.779
you learn about how to fix the city and make it better. All right,

30:34.811 --> 30:38.761
so those questions are, are we understaffed or overstaffed

30:39.261 --> 30:43.257
in any of the areas? What services do other cities provide, and could we maybe

30:43.757 --> 30:46.837
make those in-house or outsource for those services?

30:47.913 --> 30:51.542
What departments do other cities have that we don't? Maybe we could

30:51.606 --> 30:54.971
rearrange or add. to make it better for growth in the

30:55.471 --> 30:59.312
future? Are there any services that we should consider outsourcing,

30:59.909 --> 31:03.218
things like plowing? And then how

31:03.718 --> 31:07.320
many employees do we need as the city continues to grow in each department and

31:07.820 --> 31:11.382
also just overall? And the last one is how does our workload compare

31:11.513 --> 31:12.457
to the other cities?

31:15.094 --> 31:17.937
And as I stated earlier, we have not yet gathered any data.

31:18.441 --> 31:21.803
So we're here— I'm here today just to get feedback and suggestions

31:22.023 --> 31:24.666
