41:27.436 --> 41:30.341
needed. And, and that might be more than Adelaide's, uh,

41:30.806 --> 41:34.401
you know, we've got lots of talented staff. So unless

41:34.497 --> 41:37.884
you think you can get it all done— so we'll work

41:37.964 --> 41:41.478
through that. Good. I think Melissa's

41:41.978 --> 41:45.924
suggestion is actually pretty excellent. I think I, I find

41:46.229 --> 41:49.565
I have a hard time embracing ULCT's value

41:49.581 --> 41:52.789
for me, frankly. And I'm going to tell you, I think that's

41:53.289 --> 41:56.782
one of those ways that they could be a real resource to the cities of

41:57.760 --> 42:01.449
Utah, to start creating an index to help us with

42:01.949 --> 42:05.779
hiring practices, to know when and how the next hire should

42:06.279 --> 42:09.532
be done. When we're— I mean, if we're going to continue at

42:09.564 --> 42:13.044
the pace that we're continuing because the legislature is pushing these initiatives with

42:13.076 --> 42:17.991
housing, et cetera, it would be helpful for them to have data-driven

42:18.491 --> 42:21.219
decisions to be able to help its members to take a look at and go,

42:21.701 --> 42:25.219
it's time, and here are the resources, here are the ways that

42:25.719 --> 42:29.073
you can accomplish that, here's what's available to you. Because I think those are

42:29.138 --> 42:31.868
meaningful things, particularly when you're talking about cities that are young.

42:33.249 --> 42:38.036
We've gone through every difficult growth

42:38.373 --> 42:41.295
that there is. And there's a lot of cities that are coming. I mean,

42:41.311 --> 42:44.911
you build a new road across the lake, we will create a new city

42:44.927 --> 42:48.219
at the south end of that lake. And it's going to happen. And as

42:48.220 --> 42:51.713
new cities continue to grow like that, it would be good for

42:52.035 --> 42:55.625
those of us who have already been through the struggle in the last 50 years

42:56.285 --> 42:58.702
to be able to share data that could help those in the future, too.

42:59.688 --> 43:02.920
Yeah. I like

43:03.420 --> 43:06.039
that for the simple fact we keep spending money on wage studies every few years.

43:06.300 --> 43:10.004
If the data's just there and updated by each city, we can go do our

43:10.020 --> 43:13.308
own study and what people cost. So great

43:13.808 --> 43:17.124
idea. I think it's been a good discussion. You have the direction we need,

43:17.220 --> 43:21.005
both you and Natalie and staff. Yeah, perfect. Thank you so much for your time

43:21.505 --> 43:24.437
and for the suggestions. Yes, and your time here with our city. Thank you very

43:24.937 --> 43:27.805
much. Yes, thank you. It's been fun. Okay,

43:28.783 --> 43:31.926
I believe Kimberly's online as well, correct? Okay.

43:32.616 --> 43:36.456
We've had some conversations on this next one's proposed policy around

43:36.488 --> 43:39.959
tax funding for— I think it says Sheriff's Office,

43:40.024 --> 43:42.000
but I think it's public safety in general. That include both?

43:42.273 --> 43:46.130
Public safety. Yeah, so that's fire and

43:46.371 --> 43:50.034
sheriff and kind of some guardrails of some ideas.

43:50.693 --> 43:54.357
I know Kimberly's had some ideas. Evan has asked

43:54.389 --> 43:58.342
for direction specifically as we move this forward, like I

43:58.583 --> 44:01.218
think I do think you had two or three slides. Is that right? And then,

44:01.459 --> 44:04.946
and really just wondering how you want to go forward.

44:05.814 --> 44:08.899
So show us what you started with, and we'll go from there.

44:09.992 --> 44:12.450
Yep, I'll be pretty quick about this.

44:13.350 --> 44:16.966
We had some instructions from City Council to put together kind

44:17.466 --> 44:21.079
of a draft ordinance that would essentially tie our property

44:21.160 --> 44:23.779
tax revenues specifically to public safety.

44:25.117 --> 44:28.754
And again, as the mayor mentioned, it's not necessarily just the

44:29.254 --> 44:32.795
sheriff's office meant to be broadly accepting of whatever happens down the road.

44:34.420 --> 44:38.945
That draft ordinance was put together over

44:39.445 --> 44:43.026
the past week or so, and that language is all there to create

44:43.526 --> 44:46.990
a public safety fund and then have certain parameters so that

44:47.377 --> 44:50.691
as property tax ebbs and flows, There's certain thresholds

44:51.033 --> 44:54.533
that we're trying to meet within that, coverage ratios that we're trying to

44:55.033 --> 44:58.907
stay within. It does create parameters to say what

44:59.020 --> 45:01.700
does qualify as a public safety expense,

45:03.070 --> 45:06.307
you know, limitations of that fund. The monies can't move from

45:06.403 --> 45:09.802
fund to fund. There needs to be annual reviews and all sorts of

45:10.302 --> 45:13.868
things like that. So it is there now for your

45:14.368 --> 45:18.040
review and for our discussion. Discussion tonight. As the mayor suggests as well,

45:18.072 --> 45:20.978
there, there are some things that we can still talk about with this that we

45:21.478 --> 45:24.783
need to kind of figure out, both something

45:24.800 --> 45:28.220
specifically with the code as far as how you want to approach it, but then

45:28.268 --> 45:31.608
also some potential alternatives if you want to look at alternatives.

45:32.683 --> 45:36.055
A couple things that I identified in here, and this is some

45:36.135 --> 45:39.989
things that you probably already know, is that just because it's code doesn't

45:40.489 --> 45:43.688
mean it can't be It could still be changed down the road. We're obviously

45:44.188 --> 45:47.617
trying to set a standard or a precedent here

45:47.666 --> 45:50.354
now where this is the future of Eagle Mountain,

45:50.403 --> 45:53.938
how we use our property taxes, but that could change down

45:54.438 --> 45:58.145
the road. This code doesn't necessarily

45:58.452 --> 46:02.138
imply that there's any other new public review process either.

46:02.962 --> 46:06.931
There's still just the budget process as far as how this is reviewed. Reviewed annually,

46:07.363 --> 46:10.969
and then other just kind of accountability things between the city manager and whatnot.

46:12.155 --> 46:15.536
And then enforcement and accountability is a little bit ambiguous,

46:15.600 --> 46:18.821
and that's just because I didn't know exactly how you wanted to handle that.

46:19.846 --> 46:23.500
What happens if we go outside the boundaries of this and things

46:24.000 --> 46:29.092
like that? So I also just kind of left this a

46:29.140 --> 46:33.051
little bit ambiguous in some respects too, just because there is a Technically,

46:33.565 --> 46:37.066
when you approach something like this, this isn't necessarily groundbreaking

46:37.098 --> 46:41.722
or revolutionary what you're asking for. And I say that because it's not to

46:41.738 --> 46:44.949
try and kind of ease your feelings to feel like this is something that can

46:44.965 --> 46:48.610
be done. It is something that certainly could be a policy of the

46:48.642 --> 46:52.046
city council, but also kind of lead into there

46:52.095 --> 46:55.129
is an alternative that is the more typical approach, and that is to make this

46:55.177 --> 46:58.699
a financial policy. So, So this is just

46:59.199 --> 47:02.111
to kind of outline for you. Again, we do it whatever way you want to

47:02.175 --> 47:04.650
do it. I just want to make sure that you're aware that this is typically

47:04.666 --> 47:07.788
how it's done with a financial policy.

47:07.836 --> 47:11.406
And the reason why you do it through a financial policy, as mentioned

47:11.906 --> 47:15.767
in these, these bullet points, these financial policies are pretty

47:15.799 --> 47:19.205
robust as they carry through the entire financial

47:19.253 --> 47:21.213
process throughout the entirety of the year.

47:22.562 --> 47:26.485
So you're, you're looking at this through not just the budgeting process but

47:26.985 --> 47:30.114
the auditing process and all of our reporting and things like that. The financial policies

47:30.614 --> 47:34.272
are baked into all of that. Um, so you're,

47:34.288 --> 47:37.516
you're looking at adding your financial policies in the budget book.

47:37.821 --> 47:41.482
You're adding it into all these different things that come to you in multiple cases

47:41.915 --> 47:44.533
throughout the year for public viewing as well.

47:45.512 --> 47:48.900
Um, but then on top of that, uh,

47:49.141 --> 47:53.254
it's still has the same kind of teeth that this ordinance

47:53.754 --> 47:57.126
would have if the ordinance, as we're looking at it

47:57.626 --> 48:01.415
right now, maintains a certain level of enforcement and accountability, because there isn't really

48:01.915 --> 48:05.197
a whole lot of enforcement accountability currently drafted into that code.

48:07.594 --> 48:11.197
So when we look at— I would treat

48:11.697 --> 48:14.946
this almost— in my mind, it helped to kind of compare this

48:15.059 --> 48:18.793
to like our employee policies and procedures manual.

48:19.295 --> 48:22.597
It's not just kind of a policy we throw out there because we

48:23.097 --> 48:26.302
just need some sort of unity on something. It is something that's meant to have

48:26.334 --> 48:29.045
some real meat to it, that is something you can hold people to,

48:30.288 --> 48:33.725
but it's not necessarily something that's in city code.

48:35.780 --> 48:39.458
Besides that, I just want to bring up— this is what the GFOA, the Government

48:39.958 --> 48:43.586
Finance Officers Association, says on financial policies. And I

48:44.086 --> 48:46.846
won't necessarily read through all of these, but you can see on the top levels

48:46.862 --> 48:50.331
there that they regularly recommend financial

48:50.831 --> 48:53.431
policies, which we do have plenty right now already as a city.

48:54.587 --> 48:59.133
But they give a lot of weight to these financial policies as good

48:59.470 --> 49:02.923
practices for municipalities across the country for

49:03.423 --> 49:07.163
the purposes of institutionalizing good financial management practices,

49:07.838 --> 49:11.216
clarifying, crystallizing strategic intent for financial management and

49:11.232 --> 49:14.036
defining boundaries and promoting long-term and strategic thinking.

49:15.604 --> 49:19.574
These financial policies are really what guide

49:19.703 --> 49:23.431
our budgeting process overall and help guide our

49:23.931 --> 49:27.599
staff and guide all of leadership management. So that is

49:27.874 --> 49:31.414
another approach. But again, if it's your preference to move

49:31.914 --> 49:34.774
forward on a city code, we can absolutely do it that way. As well.

49:35.421 --> 49:39.057
So that's all I have for you, but happy to

49:39.768 --> 49:42.920
talk through this at all or, or take any direction from you.

49:47.137 --> 49:50.545
This is going to sound negative, but I'm just going to say it.

49:51.113 --> 49:55.007
I feel like that when we, when we talk about something that's new,

49:55.653 --> 49:58.899
we start with, well, a new council can change that. I've heard that at

49:58.947 --> 50:02.627
least 5 times. In the last month, and I'm curious

50:03.127 --> 50:06.242
why that's the first response from our staff. I've heard it from our attorney.

50:06.500 --> 50:09.681
I've heard it from you. I've heard it from Ben. I think that we're well

50:09.730 --> 50:12.527
aware of that. I don't think that needs to be stated anymore. I think that's

50:13.027 --> 50:15.346
clear. The second thing is,

50:16.843 --> 50:21.038
is I'm trying to understand why present an alternative to this because I

50:21.538 --> 50:24.909
think everybody is aware of the teeth kicking that we've taken over the last month.

50:25.977 --> 50:28.964
And when we're trying to do something that is very transparent,

50:29.525 --> 50:33.620
that shows all of our hand to the citizens to show exactly

50:34.120 --> 50:36.831
what we're trying to accomplish, and then we're told, well, we really ought to do

50:37.331 --> 50:40.107
this in a way that is kind of there, but you have to really look

50:40.607 --> 50:43.832
to find it. I just— that seems opposite

50:44.332 --> 50:46.995
of what we've been trying to accomplish with this. It's the reason that Zach and

50:47.011 --> 50:50.576
I had this conversation about forcing the issue.

50:51.090 --> 50:54.487
To show the citizens that we intend it to go exactly where we say it's

50:54.987 --> 50:58.493
going to go, and that by ordinance it's forced to go into that place.

50:59.374 --> 51:02.740
When we talk about policies, I mean, I think everybody— well, except for maybe

51:02.964 --> 51:06.553
the council members to my left and right— were here when we were told

51:07.291 --> 51:10.672
about what our policies and procedures manual actually had in it,

51:10.896 --> 51:14.053
as opposed to what we keep being told is in there. And I don't feel

51:14.117 --> 51:18.732
confident that it has all the policies that we think that

51:19.232 --> 51:19.651
are in there. I think we have to go and search to find them.

51:20.294 --> 51:24.404
And so for me, I'm not interested really in chasing after the alternative.

51:24.468 --> 51:27.696
I think those should be there also, by the way. You should have policies that

51:28.196 --> 51:31.501
are in place that are guiding how you do those things. But with respect to

51:31.517 --> 51:35.355
how we're going to evaluate property tax specifically,

51:36.350 --> 51:39.786
that should be crystal clear. And I think that we have

51:40.286 --> 51:43.255
told everybody that this is what our plan was. There's a lot of people who

51:43.755 --> 51:46.869
are skeptical as to whether or not that's actually the case. And I want it

51:47.369 --> 51:50.949
to be 100% clear that when we said that's what it was, we intended for

51:50.965 --> 51:53.985
that to be the case. And I want it to be hard.

51:54.483 --> 51:56.619
I hope a council has to step up and go, yeah, you know what,

51:56.667 --> 52:00.121
we really don't want you to know where we're putting this money, and let them

52:00.621 --> 52:04.281
take that fire. Good luck with that. But I'm going to tell you, I think

52:04.781 --> 52:07.863
that public safety, if we're going to go there, it should be restricted to that.

52:08.040 --> 52:10.715
So that we do exactly what we say we're doing. That's my opinion.

52:13.915 --> 52:17.534
Thanks. Thank you, Evan, for preparing this. I'm probably

52:17.566 --> 52:19.075
in the camp of do both.

52:21.320 --> 52:25.397
Code and having it written in

52:25.897 --> 52:29.280
our city code allows for residents to come to a city council

52:29.780 --> 52:32.995
and say, it says right here that this is how this should work.

52:33.570 --> 52:37.019
I understand we can't bind a future council to

52:37.115 --> 52:39.698
decrease tax rates or increase tax rates.

52:40.740 --> 52:43.949
The idea of this mechanism, having that in

52:44.449 --> 52:48.087
code, is that it would require that councils would, in an open meeting,

52:48.617 --> 52:52.483
talk about what is our coverage ratio, with the target

52:52.852 --> 52:56.654
being stated at 85%. If the

52:57.729 --> 53:02.107
coverage ratio they meet, it's at 100%, then the council could consider

53:02.620 --> 53:06.483
cutting the tax, pulling it back. Or if it's

53:06.983 --> 53:10.748
below the lower band of 75%, then maybe a

53:11.248 --> 53:14.980
modest increase to property tax is warranted to catch up

53:15.480 --> 53:18.827
to the growth that we've had in sheriffs. Not having

53:19.327 --> 53:24.117
that for the last 10 years has created this environment where we have had our

53:24.759 --> 53:28.353
safety subsidized by sales taxes. Sales taxes, which, which as we all know,

53:28.369 --> 53:32.190
can fluctuate with the economy. This just assures that city councils

53:33.009 --> 53:36.478
in a public meeting can review the coverage ratio, look at the upper

53:36.978 --> 53:40.460
bands and lower bands, and the second piece of that is is

53:40.556 --> 53:44.393
having those funds assured that they're not commingled

53:44.891 --> 53:48.424
with with sales tax revenue. That when you pay a property tax,

53:48.440 --> 53:51.699
you know it's going in exactly for public safety.

53:52.358 --> 53:55.361
Today, that would be for our sheriff's contract.

53:56.023 --> 53:59.510
So I think I'm probably both. Let's hit it on both sides.

53:59.558 --> 54:02.730
I think it's a great idea to have a financial policy, but I'd also like

54:03.230 --> 54:07.102
to have it in code. So for future councils,

54:07.151 --> 54:09.999
it's there, and I think we're more likely to adhere to it.

54:12.138 --> 54:15.350
And again, thank you. I read through it. You spent a lot of time putting

54:15.850 --> 54:19.394
that together. It's very good. No. I really like the

54:19.410 --> 54:22.842
idea of having it in both, um, because I— as

54:23.515 --> 54:27.332
we were having this kind of discussion about code versus policy,

54:28.070 --> 54:31.999
we've had code in the past where we had like 3 different boards

54:32.499 --> 54:35.286
and commissions that just existed in code, but we didn't do anything with them.

54:35.767 --> 54:39.519
And it took finally repealing that, that like code

54:39.616 --> 54:42.887
is more visible for residents, but it doesn't necessarily

54:42.935 --> 54:46.372
mean that something's Done with it. I think that we're making changes

54:46.872 --> 54:50.437
in that direction, but I like the policy because everybody

54:50.469 --> 54:53.490
knows that the financial nerds love their policy,

54:54.148 --> 54:57.298
and they will. It's just like the timing

54:57.314 --> 55:01.074
of when each report is due and all of those other things. That's all they

55:01.574 --> 55:05.750
live and die by that, and I think that's a really important piece. I also

55:05.878 --> 55:09.847
wrote down who's responsible to execute this. Is it?

55:09.992 --> 55:13.678
Under the city manager's responsibility, is it the director of a certain

55:13.744 --> 55:17.386
functional area? I think that that's a really important

55:17.418 --> 55:19.500
piece that we need to determine,

55:21.320 --> 55:24.829
so that we both the public and the council

55:25.329 --> 55:28.711
and mayor can hold this person accountable to make sure that they're

55:29.211 --> 55:32.714
bringing us the information. And then there

55:32.747 --> 55:35.934
were some other things that I talked to a couple council members about.

55:37.117 --> 55:40.708
And I don't know if it needs to go on this or if it needs

55:40.740 --> 55:44.668
to go in a different piece of code, but I know staff

55:45.168 --> 55:48.627
has been working on some level of service metrics. And I

55:48.675 --> 55:52.362
think that we need to ask for some specific

55:52.426 --> 55:55.649
metrics required in code to be brought

55:56.149 --> 56:00.346
to council and presented in our first like budget meeting when we first

56:00.939 --> 56:05.111
start having our strategic meetings

56:05.611 --> 56:09.576
and different things like that. And that would be things like call volume and

56:09.833 --> 56:13.543
how much the contract is, and, but it would also be

56:14.667 --> 56:18.007
acres of parks. So it would be a full budget thing,

56:18.136 --> 56:21.717
not just for public safety with the metrics. And then the other thing that I

56:22.217 --> 56:26.278
think that we need to work to provide for our residents is

56:26.792 --> 56:31.256
clarity and kind of, Making it simple,

56:32.236 --> 56:36.107
and I really liked how we had the budget in brief. I think

56:36.607 --> 56:40.284
we should always create a budget in brief, and it should be prepared

56:40.784 --> 56:44.460
and presented the same time that the preliminary budget

56:44.960 --> 56:48.556
is presented, and it should be no more than like ten pages. It should

56:49.056 --> 56:52.973
list out the capital projects. It should list out all of the

56:53.198 --> 56:56.961
employees that are being you know. Prepared for in the budget and any

56:57.074 --> 57:01.614
like major changes. So I

57:01.742 --> 57:05.236
think the metrics over a 5-year thing, all of those things are in

57:05.349 --> 57:09.665
the budget. Our staff has been adding them to the reports,

57:10.261 --> 57:13.901
but having it required to be presented to

57:14.401 --> 57:18.632
the public, I think is a really good move in reestablishing

57:18.648 --> 57:23.441
trust and helping them understand that we've heard You

57:23.941 --> 57:25.774
made me think of something when you said that, by the way. I think you

57:26.274 --> 57:30.254
have a good idea. When it's presented, so who should

57:30.754 --> 57:34.429
be responsible for it? I think it's probably gonna be part of the report when

57:34.929 --> 57:38.520
they bring the budget to us. Perhaps you need to show us the percent

57:39.116 --> 57:43.369
is really what it needs to come down to. So here's what the current contract

57:43.869 --> 57:48.126
is, here's what the current collected, uh, Property tax is

57:48.399 --> 57:51.996
that was collected. Here's what percentage of the

57:52.496 --> 57:55.785
bands that that is, so that it causes the discussion to have to happen anyway.

57:56.347 --> 57:59.543
But it also gives us some visibility. So I think maybe baking that into the

58:00.043 --> 58:03.541
code probably ought to be there. One of the things that I think when Melissa

58:03.557 --> 58:07.764
was talking, it made me think also: we need to start the practice of evaluating

58:07.828 --> 58:11.376
an end year year after year budget.

58:11.730 --> 58:15.449
We need to be able to compare to our previous years. And if there's a

58:15.949 --> 58:19.104
financial cost of doing that, we need to do it. Because not knowing

58:19.216 --> 58:23.192
how we're spending department by department, and especially

58:23.224 --> 58:26.847
depleted funds, whether or not we're using the budget, we need to know

58:27.536 --> 58:31.207
whether or not we're meeting those goals, we're overspending, we're underspending,

58:31.864 --> 58:35.087
we're under-budgeted for some departments, we're way

58:35.587 --> 58:38.910
over-budgeted for other departments, I think is part of the budgeting process

58:39.733 --> 58:43.202
to make us better at making those decisions. We need to know that

58:43.702 --> 58:46.642
information, but we also need to be able to compare it with how we're doing

58:46.739 --> 58:49.886
year after year. So I'd like that to be baked in there as well.

58:53.740 --> 58:57.346
I think— sorry, I think that all of them apply

58:57.492 --> 59:01.114
to the budget. I think that the clarity that

59:01.614 --> 59:05.286
we're asking for with public safety is is really important. I'm not sure

59:05.430 --> 59:09.237
if it needs to be 2 completely different sections of code or

59:09.285 --> 59:12.642
if it's another section added on to the end of

59:13.142 --> 59:16.690
it and it's just overreaching budget. I think

59:17.190 --> 59:20.657
separate. I would have, after we work on this,

59:21.091 --> 59:24.785
we move to the next part of what does that financial reporting look

59:25.285 --> 59:28.785
like so it is crystal clear when we put that budget together

59:28.801 --> 59:32.053
that layman can— how often, when does it come,

59:32.166 --> 59:35.354
how are things being paid for? I would propose that we do that as a

59:35.402 --> 59:36.691
second piece of code.

59:40.952 --> 59:44.268
I like it, Evan. And I mean, I want to thank Councilmember Hewish

59:44.655 --> 59:48.084
and Councilmember— I want to call him Councilmember Rich

59:48.132 --> 59:52.216
because Wood— I couldn't think of it. But Councilmember Wood for

59:52.330 --> 59:55.856
their leadership in this. It makes a lot of sense to me,

59:57.321 --> 01:00:01.380
and especially after the butt-kicking we've been taking, taking in

01:00:01.412 --> 01:00:04.959
the public eye for the statements we've made that property

01:00:05.459 --> 01:00:08.902
taxes are going to be dedicated to the sheriff's contract. They are in fact going

01:00:09.016 --> 01:00:12.915
to be dedicated to that wholly. But I think this helps bring

01:00:12.948 --> 01:00:16.916
that transparency that our citizens have been asking for. It also

01:00:17.416 --> 01:00:20.905
makes it easier for future councils, for any resident and any staff

01:00:21.405 --> 01:00:24.649
member to be able to find it easily because I know the policies and procedures

01:00:24.665 --> 01:00:28.040
manual exists. Just out of curiosity, how many of the

01:00:28.540 --> 01:00:31.887
council members have a copy of the policies and procedures manual that's current?

01:00:34.524 --> 01:00:39.077
How many of you would know where to find it? So that's

01:00:39.577 --> 01:00:42.882
at least in my way of thinking one of the reasons

01:00:43.382 --> 01:00:46.916
why I think both make sense. I think that The policies and procedures manual

01:00:47.416 --> 01:00:50.095
works great for staff. I don't think it works great for the elected officials.

01:00:51.251 --> 01:00:53.997
I do have a copy. I don't know if it's the most recent,

01:00:54.543 --> 01:00:58.204
but I saved it and I've gone through it on multiple occasions,

01:00:58.236 --> 01:01:01.624
but I've been here a little while. And so that's why I asked the question,

01:01:01.672 --> 01:01:05.108
not to embarrass anyone, but to just create that transparency.

01:01:05.156 --> 01:01:08.191
So I think that it makes a lot of sense for this to land itself

01:01:08.691 --> 01:01:11.531
in code. It's really easy for anybody that wants to find it,

01:01:12.029 --> 01:01:15.661
who's willing to get into the EMMC, right? And that's

01:01:16.161 --> 01:01:19.549
not always easy, but it's certainly available if we're willing to put forward

01:01:19.839 --> 01:01:23.177
the effort to find it. And I'm supportive of doing

01:01:23.225 --> 01:01:26.857
both strategies as well, and I'm grateful for these 2 council members at my left

01:01:27.357 --> 01:01:31.135
that recognize the issue and were proactive about

01:01:31.635 --> 01:01:34.768
trying to solve the issue going forward. I think this provides a significant level of

01:01:35.268 --> 01:01:38.992
transparency for the citizens And makes it a lot easier on future councils to stay

01:01:39.492 --> 01:01:40.650
up with this particular issue.

01:01:42.640 --> 01:01:46.062
I'll just— so maybe some

01:01:46.562 --> 01:01:49.340
direction. I would just say as a new council member coming in, this is a

01:01:49.840 --> 01:01:53.802
very easy policy to digest for someone who has never worked in government before.

01:01:54.336 --> 01:01:57.639
For those that have many years of experience,

01:01:57.655 --> 01:02:02.138
for someone new coming in, it's, I think, a really digestible

01:02:02.187 --> 01:02:05.883
policy. Any feedback for Evan before—

01:02:06.127 --> 01:02:09.221
for him to come back before we actually put this into code?

01:02:10.870 --> 01:02:14.251
No, I think, I think if you add some accountability metrics and some timing

01:02:14.283 --> 01:02:16.972
and some of those things, and if you guys want to work through that and

01:02:17.326 --> 01:02:20.208
help him wordsmith it and bring it back to us, I'm okay with that.

01:02:20.256 --> 01:02:24.317
I don't know how everybody else feels. I think

01:02:24.817 --> 01:02:27.788
this is step one. I think everybody agrees that this should be the first step.

01:02:29.536 --> 01:02:33.116
I think a full budget policy is smart that is

01:02:33.148 --> 01:02:36.922
done by ordinance. If we can deal with the property tax portion first,

01:02:37.500 --> 01:02:41.016
to alliterate to the residents, it'll be

01:02:41.516 --> 01:02:44.083
in its own separate account. I think that's item one that needs to be clear.

01:02:44.468 --> 01:02:47.567
It cannot be used for anything else but public safety.

01:02:48.322 --> 01:02:52.143
Number 2, we need to show how we're coming up with the determination of

01:02:52.175 --> 01:02:55.708
whether or not it needs to be revisited. With a property tax increase or

01:02:56.208 --> 01:02:59.465
decrease, so that needs to be done when the report comes back to

01:02:59.965 --> 01:03:03.367
future councils and even this next year. That the property tax,

01:03:03.865 --> 01:03:07.429
excuse me, collected with our contracts that

01:03:07.929 --> 01:03:11.572
are going to be drawing from that, what is that percentage? That that's something

01:03:11.588 --> 01:03:15.939
that's presented as part of the budget process. And then I think that's

01:03:16.439 --> 01:03:19.215
good on the property tax portion of it. I think the language that you have

01:03:19.715 --> 01:03:23.065
is actually. Actually great on this one. But I think that there are other aspects

01:03:23.565 --> 01:03:26.675
that we've kind of talked about that probably need to be the next portion that

01:03:27.175 --> 01:03:30.782
we work on. We need to deal with how we ebb and flow with our

01:03:31.282 --> 01:03:34.792
variable revenue and how we determine how

01:03:35.292 --> 01:03:38.787
much of that we can give from that variable

01:03:39.287 --> 01:03:42.782
revenue. And I'm not talking about restricted funds, I'm talking specifically about sales

01:03:42.878 --> 01:03:46.263
tax, whether or not we can afford to give

01:03:46.763 --> 01:03:50.721
sales tax to the property tax to be able to make up that extra 15%,

01:03:50.994 --> 01:03:54.297
as it might be. And I think policies that are in place

01:03:54.345 --> 01:03:57.793
for that, I would find it very strange for a city council

01:03:58.293 --> 01:04:01.353
to come in and change something that's actually helping them, frankly.

01:04:01.738 --> 01:04:06.036
And I think this creates a level of simplicity

01:04:06.709 --> 01:04:09.772
in taking a look at this and being able to eat it in chunks.

01:04:10.302 --> 01:04:13.684
But I think one of the things I've heard very most from citizens is it's

01:04:14.184 --> 01:04:16.777
not clear how to even determine where it's going.

01:04:17.610 --> 01:04:20.559
And I think that ought to be clear. And I'm going to tell you,

01:04:20.575 --> 01:04:23.604
I mean, I've been doing this for 2 and a half years and, you know,

01:04:23.684 --> 01:04:27.097
4-something years before that. And there are parts of this I'm like,

01:04:27.866 --> 01:04:30.270
I think this is where it is. And we have to ask a lot of

01:04:30.770 --> 01:04:34.292
questions to get the clarity. I think we need to make it a little simpler

01:04:34.741 --> 01:04:38.910
to digest from anybody who wants to to know and have the access

01:04:39.410 --> 01:04:41.897
for them to be able to see it truly is going where we say it's

01:04:42.397 --> 01:04:45.879
going. Maybe it's not easy for us to explain that because it is a very

01:04:45.911 --> 01:04:50.005
complicated accounting metric, but I think our code ought to be able to outline

01:04:50.505 --> 01:04:53.907
it. I like what Melissa was saying, it should be able to be broken down

01:04:54.582 --> 01:04:58.451
in a way that the public can digest easily to know exactly how

01:04:58.951 --> 01:05:02.144
much is going to each of our departments and then how we're performing.

01:05:02.192 --> 01:05:05.886
Thank you. Yeah, I would echo that. I think if

01:05:06.386 --> 01:05:09.483
there's one consistent message, maybe not everybody,

01:05:09.595 --> 01:05:13.224
but I would say the majority, people support our public safety.

01:05:13.851 --> 01:05:17.480
And for me to look at my property tax and think that's securing my

01:05:17.980 --> 01:05:21.302
safety, 100% of those dollars are going to fund the Sheriff's

01:05:21.802 --> 01:05:25.493
Office, that, that's a win. That's very easy to explain for everyone. Do you

01:05:25.993 --> 01:05:29.122
have— need any other direction, Evan, or have any questions? I was just— sorry,

01:05:29.170 --> 01:05:32.077
go ahead. I wanted to jump in Direction-wise to help,

01:05:33.281 --> 01:05:36.316
I think you and I are the only ones I've talked to about this.

01:05:36.412 --> 01:05:39.864
I don't remember if I've talked to anyone else. Maybe I did, but we

01:05:40.364 --> 01:05:42.578
were kind of spitballing, and I think I was the one that said, "Well,

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:46.640
maybe we do that 10% thing up and down," and you liked it.

01:05:47.395 --> 01:05:50.702
But we just used 85 as an example,

01:05:50.718 --> 01:05:53.785
so I didn't know if I'm not saying it's not

01:05:53.833 --> 01:05:57.413
a good number. I didn't know if the council said. 85 is the number.

01:05:57.461 --> 01:06:01.039
So I mean, if we— is that based on a metrics? Is that based

01:06:01.539 --> 01:06:05.707
on— I just want to make sure that we're kind of— yeah,

01:06:05.724 --> 01:06:09.526
I think I actually did. I do like 85%.

01:06:10.344 --> 01:06:13.521
If you set it at 100 and you go to 110,

01:06:13.553 --> 01:06:16.906
you've now spent a year over-collecting. And then

01:06:16.938 --> 01:06:19.922
what do you do with those funds and how do you appropriate them?

01:06:19.986 --> 01:06:23.683
But if you're at 85%, it's seems like it would be very difficult

01:06:24.183 --> 01:06:27.871
to get to 100 or drop down to 60. And, you know, wherever we

01:06:28.371 --> 01:06:32.195
end up landing in our, in our later session tonight, I think 85% is

01:06:32.244 --> 01:06:36.118
a reasonable coverage ratio. You wouldn't be supplementing too

01:06:36.618 --> 01:06:37.999
much with sales tax year to year.

01:06:42.206 --> 01:06:45.739
Yeah, I would tend to agree with that, Councilmember Hewish. And I, I just,

01:06:45.934 --> 01:06:49.483
for the, just for the general public

01:06:49.983 --> 01:06:50.927
to kind of get a sense of this. This is a shift in policy,

01:06:51.681 --> 01:06:55.177
and I want that to be very clear. And it's a shift in policy

01:06:55.273 --> 01:06:58.817
that we as a council have kind of said we think

01:06:59.317 --> 01:07:02.971
this is a better way to govern. And we know we've been

01:07:02.987 --> 01:07:06.964
beat up because of it, but I still truly believe that. I believe that

01:07:07.060 --> 01:07:10.653
funding our public safety needs, at least the majority

01:07:10.749 --> 01:07:13.812
of it, through property tax revenue is is it

01:07:13.876 --> 01:07:17.245
the most stable and reliable source of

01:07:17.745 --> 01:07:20.855
income that we have as a city, and it ensures that we'll be able

01:07:20.871 --> 01:07:24.512
to meet those expectations that we have upon ourselves and upon

01:07:24.545 --> 01:07:28.154
our citizens. So I just think that that's a really important

01:07:28.170 --> 01:07:32.004
part of this puzzle that has perhaps been kicked aside and

01:07:32.101 --> 01:07:35.646
removed from the equation. I hope people understand that it is a shift

01:07:36.146 --> 01:07:39.609
in policy. The previous policy has been, we'll see whatever sells,

01:07:39.641 --> 01:07:43.542
we'll We'll take whatever property tax there is, and then we're going to take funds

01:07:43.558 --> 01:07:47.059
from any other source that we can gather it, and we're just going to deal

01:07:47.107 --> 01:07:50.543
with the growth in public safety, and we'll figure the rest out.

01:07:50.800 --> 01:07:54.156
We want to be more proactive and deliberative in the

01:07:54.656 --> 01:07:57.945
results that we're producing, and I know that that's painful. And so I

01:07:58.009 --> 01:08:01.397
just want that out there for people to hear. I also

01:08:01.897 --> 01:08:05.749
think that we've talked about a lot of really important puzzle pieces. We talked

01:08:06.249 --> 01:08:10.437
about the staffing study and getting that kind of an index and understanding

01:08:10.453 --> 01:08:14.853
where we rank with other cities. We've talked about earmarking

01:08:14.949 --> 01:08:19.413
funds very specifically and clearly making it designated

01:08:19.461 --> 01:08:23.395
for public safety. We've talked about accountability. We've talked

01:08:23.895 --> 01:08:27.569
about simplicity and helping our residents know what's going on. And I

01:08:28.069 --> 01:08:31.715
think— Thank you. There's always

01:08:31.731 --> 01:08:35.066
room for improvement, and I definitely think that this— we're headed in the

01:08:35.566 --> 01:08:37.741
right direction. So thanks, you guys, for working on it.

01:08:39.862 --> 01:08:43.290
I just want to share my appreciation to the, the council for,

01:08:43.339 --> 01:08:45.716
uh, your feedback and your direction on this. I think, uh,

01:08:46.961 --> 01:08:49.825
where I kind of missed on this was I was really focused on the,

01:08:50.441 --> 01:08:54.155
the creation and the implementation part of it, and I heard from all of you

01:08:54.188 --> 01:08:58.587
about the need for it to be transparent and something that can be easily seen

01:08:58.619 --> 01:09:02.222
by the public, and that's where the code comes

01:09:02.254 --> 01:09:06.828
really in the front of mind. So I

01:09:06.860 --> 01:09:10.191
understand that, and I think that's— it makes a lot of sense.

01:09:10.272 --> 01:09:13.489
So I'll take what feedback you've gotten, and we'll make some tweaks.

01:09:19.105 --> 01:09:22.465
Thank you. And just real quick on that one, you're good, Evan.

01:09:22.513 --> 01:09:25.739
I just wanted to make sure Kimberly, I know you had some thoughts. That's why

01:09:26.239 --> 01:09:28.470
I checked online if she's available.

01:09:29.852 --> 01:09:33.515
Does that— do you feel like that direction— I feel

01:09:34.015 --> 01:09:35.990
like you're the one that's going to have to implement this. So do you have

01:09:36.490 --> 01:09:39.251
any input? Is that fair? No,

01:09:39.749 --> 01:09:43.637
I just want to reiterate that, you know, as staff, always we are—

01:09:45.035 --> 01:09:48.666
you know, our goal is to identify these policies and the things that the

01:09:49.166 --> 01:09:52.089
council wants and to find a way to bring that forward. So again, the things

01:09:52.121 --> 01:09:55.640
that we do are to make it clearer and better for you guys to do

01:09:56.140 --> 01:09:59.785
the job that you've been elected to do. And so I'll

01:10:00.058 --> 01:10:03.979
continue to work with just Evan offline as he continues

01:10:04.011 --> 01:10:08.060
to flesh that out, and we'll

01:10:08.108 --> 01:10:11.530
continue to make efforts to improve that clarity

01:10:12.028 --> 01:10:16.592
and make sure that our residents

01:10:17.092 --> 01:10:20.537
as well as the council can answer some easy, you know, questions that should maybe

01:10:20.602 --> 01:10:22.678
be easy for you to be able to identify in our processes.

01:10:26.310 --> 01:10:29.852
Okay, perfect. Thank you. We've got a lot to

01:10:30.352 --> 01:10:33.539
get through and not a lot of time, so I hope— I know there's

01:10:34.039 --> 01:10:36.624
at least a couple more discussions that we want to have before we break out

01:10:37.124 --> 01:10:40.897
for a closed session. There are a couple of items that Marcus can update

01:10:41.397 --> 01:10:45.100
as well, and I think So next item was an opportunity

01:10:45.149 --> 01:10:48.889
for the city manager to fill us in on— I

01:10:49.389 --> 01:10:52.188
just appreciate the mayor and the staff for covering while I was out last week.

01:10:52.349 --> 01:10:55.547
And I know a lot of— obviously from the work session, a lot of good

01:10:55.579 --> 01:10:59.260
things and good ideas were implemented in my absence. And a

01:10:59.839 --> 01:11:03.242
lot of good things happened in general in the

01:11:03.742 --> 01:11:06.907
community. I know it's been a painful week, and I just want to express my

01:11:07.407 --> 01:11:11.704
appreciation. To the council for the leadership that you're— the example

01:11:11.848 --> 01:11:15.506
that you're creating for our entire community. And I just want to stress

01:11:16.100 --> 01:11:19.822
the importance of having civility. It's okay to disagree. It's not

01:11:20.322 --> 01:11:24.073
okay to be disagreeable to the— and especially to the extent that

01:11:24.573 --> 01:11:28.486
it becomes personal. And I think every single—

01:11:28.534 --> 01:11:32.304
there's not an elected leader I've worked with over my entire

01:11:32.804 --> 01:11:36.237
career that doesn't get into it because they want to make their

01:11:36.737 --> 01:11:40.557
community better and they love their community. And they might see things

01:11:40.589 --> 01:11:44.828
differently, they might see different directions

01:11:45.328 --> 01:11:49.083
on how things should proceed, but the commonality between every single one

01:11:49.179 --> 01:11:51.909
of them is that they love their community. And I know that you love your

01:11:52.409 --> 01:11:55.281
community and you want to see what's best for it. And I just want to

01:11:55.781 --> 01:11:59.552
stress that to our residents. You both All

01:12:01.964 --> 01:12:05.266
right, thanks. As we go through this, I just wanted to touch

01:12:05.363 --> 01:12:08.341
on some things to make sure that— I think this is an easier time than

01:12:08.373 --> 01:12:11.955
later. When we come to the public comment, I think I've talked to a lot

01:12:11.987 --> 01:12:15.876
of people throughout the week, but leaving the public comment

01:12:15.940 --> 01:12:19.239
open— I know right now we're going to try to have a discussion on some

01:12:19.287 --> 01:12:23.698
of the items in our next meeting, but that we save

01:12:23.795 --> 01:12:27.255
or leave open the public comment for some of those items that I think

01:12:27.755 --> 01:12:30.970
that people might be here to speak about. It's something that we've done in the

01:12:31.035 --> 01:12:34.883
past. I just wanted to make sure if there's any hesitation that we talk about

01:12:35.336 --> 01:12:38.247
it now. You okay with that? Yeah. Okay,

01:12:38.958 --> 01:12:42.678
we'll handle that when we get to it. The consent

01:12:43.178 --> 01:12:46.899
agenda item 9A, the towns at Briley Farms into warranty.

01:12:47.708 --> 01:12:51.474
