01:20:31.608 --> 01:20:34.762
All right, thank you. Um, hope everybody can hear me okay, and thanks

01:20:35.262 --> 01:20:38.735
for allowing me to be here online. Um, what I'd

01:20:38.751 --> 01:20:42.536
like to do is just really quickly go through what we do as auditors,

01:20:42.569 --> 01:20:45.545
the conclusions that we came to, and how we came to those conclusions.

01:20:47.058 --> 01:20:51.331
So there's 3 areas that we look at. We want to make sure the financial

01:20:51.363 --> 01:20:54.641
statements are materially correct, that they meet accounting standards, and those

01:20:54.673 --> 01:20:58.738
numbers can be relied upon. We want to make sure internal controls are effective,

01:20:59.236 --> 01:21:02.835
that they're designed, implemented, and effectively working.

01:21:03.911 --> 01:21:06.466
And we want to make sure you're in compliance with state law based on the

01:21:06.482 --> 01:21:10.177
areas we looked at during that fiscal year. As far as the financial

01:21:10.677 --> 01:21:13.583
statements go, we want to make sure that they're materially correct.

01:21:15.783 --> 01:21:19.411
In our opinion paragraph, it says,

01:21:19.475 --> 01:21:23.264
in our opinion, the financial statements referred to above present fairly in

01:21:23.764 --> 01:21:27.807
all material respects the respective financial position of the governmental activities,

01:21:27.840 --> 01:21:31.500
business-type activities, each major fund, and the aggregate remaining fund information

01:21:32.576 --> 01:21:36.397
of the city as of June 30th, 2025, and the respective changes in financial

01:21:36.429 --> 01:21:39.736
position where applicable, cash flows there for the year then

01:21:39.864 --> 01:21:43.091
ended, in accordance with accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America.

01:21:44.190 --> 01:21:47.856
That's considered an unmodified opinion, a clean opinion, or the

01:21:48.356 --> 01:21:51.968
best opinion that you can receive. So how we came to that conclusion,

01:21:52.653 --> 01:21:55.228
we take the trial balance of the city and then we perform a number of

01:21:55.261 --> 01:21:56.288
tests on those balances.

01:21:58.502 --> 01:22:01.842
We look at the trial balance, we review it to make sure it's accurate,

01:22:02.068 --> 01:22:04.489
that it's accurate,

01:22:04.795 --> 01:22:08.656
clerically accurate. We send out a lot of confirmations. So we'll

01:22:09.156 --> 01:22:11.372
send out confirmations to banks. confirming cash.

01:22:12.882 --> 01:22:16.434
County for property taxes, State Tax Commission for

01:22:16.934 --> 01:22:20.148
sales tax and other taxes ran through their department.

01:22:21.569 --> 01:22:25.491
We look— we send confirmations to URS for retirement services

01:22:25.765 --> 01:22:29.089
and pension obligations. And then other

01:22:29.153 --> 01:22:32.691
confirmations for debt and things that we

01:22:33.191 --> 01:22:36.599
feel are necessary. We do a lot of review of invoices.

01:22:37.068 --> 01:22:39.806
So at the beginning of the audit, we'll pull a sample of invoices. We'll look

01:22:40.306 --> 01:22:44.124
through those to make sure that they're appropriately approved, that it's— the

01:22:44.624 --> 01:22:47.261
check matches the invoice, that it's posted the proper period,

01:22:48.225 --> 01:22:51.631
and posted to the proper GL account.

01:22:52.740 --> 01:22:55.552
We do that at the beginning of the audit. Now we just randomly pull a

01:22:56.052 --> 01:22:58.802
sample of invoices and check those items.

01:22:59.918 --> 01:23:03.702
We do it throughout the course of the audit when we're looking at capital

01:23:04.202 --> 01:23:07.612
assets, We pull a number of invoices after the end of the year to make

01:23:08.112 --> 01:23:10.984
sure that the expense was recorded in the proper period.

01:23:12.012 --> 01:23:15.641
So we'll pull a number of invoices in July and August to make sure

01:23:16.141 --> 01:23:20.265
that those expenses that were in June were actually recorded in June.

01:23:21.244 --> 01:23:25.724
We do a lot of recalculation of balances as far as accrued

01:23:25.756 --> 01:23:28.775
payroll, depreciation expense, compensated absences,

01:23:28.984 --> 01:23:31.745
accrued interest. pension allocations,

01:23:32.564 --> 01:23:35.695
and we'll go back and make sure that those calculations meet accounting standards and they're

01:23:36.195 --> 01:23:39.854
appropriate for the financial statements. And then we do a lot of analytical reviews.

01:23:39.950 --> 01:23:43.386
We'll look at this year compared to last year, create expectations,

01:23:44.462 --> 01:23:47.561
and then measure the results against those expectations.

01:23:48.958 --> 01:23:52.828
If there are differences, then we start to inquire and perform procedures to make sure

01:23:53.328 --> 01:23:56.441
we can understand the differences and what happened throughout the year. We do that at

01:23:56.941 --> 01:24:00.820
the beginning of the audit. We also do that as we're doing individual balance testing.

01:24:01.964 --> 01:24:04.183
And then we look at that at the end of the audit as well,

01:24:04.896 --> 01:24:08.768
so that we can make sure that we can understand what happened as, as we

01:24:09.268 --> 01:24:13.251
look at the financial statements on a high level. There's a lot of other

01:24:13.751 --> 01:24:17.327
tests and procedures that we perform on the financial statements to make

01:24:17.376 --> 01:24:20.465
sure that they do meet accounting standards and can be relied upon.

01:24:21.730 --> 01:24:25.713
Second thing we do is we look at internal controls. We don't necessarily

01:24:25.729 --> 01:24:29.103
give an opinion on controls. We do evaluate those controls to make

01:24:29.603 --> 01:24:33.038
sure that they are designed, implemented, and working effectively. And we do

01:24:33.538 --> 01:24:36.058
that through interviews, walkthroughs, questionnaires.

01:24:36.732 --> 01:24:40.057
We, as I mentioned, we sample those invoices and we'll

01:24:40.427 --> 01:24:43.961
walk those invoices through the process to make sure that they go

01:24:44.461 --> 01:24:47.364
from the source document all the way to the financial statements and those and controls

01:24:47.864 --> 01:24:51.912
are being followed. As we did do our evaluation

01:24:51.928 --> 01:24:55.332
of internal controls, there's one finding that we felt we

01:24:55.832 --> 01:24:59.260
needed to bring to your attention. As we were

01:24:59.646 --> 01:25:03.494
reconciling, or as we were going through our procedures, we noted that certain

01:25:03.510 --> 01:25:05.748
reconciliations were not happening on a timely basis.

01:25:07.295 --> 01:25:10.719
We feel the cause of that was due to an untimely turnover in the

01:25:11.219 --> 01:25:15.087
finance department, which created delays. improper recording and reconciling of those accounts,

01:25:17.084 --> 01:25:20.708
and the complexity of maintaining those assets on a worksheet rather than

01:25:21.208 --> 01:25:25.390
an asset management software system. The recommendation

01:25:25.890 --> 01:25:29.396
is that we, the city, put in procedures to ensure that those capital

01:25:29.444 --> 01:25:33.163
assets are recorded properly and that

01:25:33.663 --> 01:25:36.850
the general— and then they're reconciled to the general ledger and depreciation schedules.

01:25:37.448 --> 01:25:41.654
And then we also recommend that the city look into an asset management

01:25:42.154 --> 01:25:44.437
system rather than a spreadsheet.

01:25:46.290 --> 01:25:49.017
So the third thing that we do is we look at state compliance.

01:25:50.390 --> 01:25:54.267
So this year we look at— we looked at budgetary

01:25:54.299 --> 01:25:57.983
compliance, fund balance, restricted taxes,

01:25:58.645 --> 01:26:01.594
fraud risk assessment, government fees, cash management,

01:26:01.692 --> 01:26:04.018
and enterprise fund transfers.

01:26:06.132 --> 01:26:09.745
What happens is the State Auditor gives us certain procedures to perform. We look

01:26:10.245 --> 01:26:13.920
at those procedures and follow those guidelines to see if you're

01:26:14.420 --> 01:26:17.741
in compliance with state law. During our review and testing,

01:26:17.918 --> 01:26:20.792
there were 2 areas where we felt that there were findings.

01:26:21.129 --> 01:26:24.999
One is the fund balance. The state requires that fund

01:26:25.499 --> 01:26:27.857
balance cannot be more than 35% of the general fund revenues.

01:26:28.611 --> 01:26:32.309
In the city, we did exceed that. The caveat

01:26:32.374 --> 01:26:36.039
is that in the state code, it includes

01:26:36.329 --> 01:26:39.978
unrestricted or unassigned fund balance and committed fund balance,

01:26:41.063 --> 01:26:44.732
where you do have significant amount of that money

01:26:44.748 --> 01:26:48.288
has been committed for streetlights. So that does play a

01:26:48.788 --> 01:26:52.298
little bit of a factor in there. The second finding

01:26:52.492 --> 01:26:56.124
was every 6 months, the state requires that

01:26:56.624 --> 01:27:00.371
we submit a deposit investment report. And we noticed that there were some

01:27:01.083 --> 01:27:05.533
inaccuracies on that report. I know that those have been addressed

01:27:06.033 --> 01:27:08.941
and they're making those changes as we speak to get those in line.

01:27:11.608 --> 01:27:15.101
I know you're busy and that was a really quick review of the financial statements.

01:27:15.890 --> 01:27:19.948
I do feel like it's worthy to note that

01:27:20.061 --> 01:27:23.893
as you look and review the financial statements, it can appear that there's a lot

01:27:24.393 --> 01:27:28.671
of revenues in excess of expenses. Of expenses, but it's

01:27:29.171 --> 01:27:32.218
good to note that of of the sixty-six million that we're showing in a change

01:27:32.718 --> 01:27:36.536
in net position, forty-six million of that was due from assets

01:27:37.036 --> 01:27:40.468
that were contributed to the city by developers: roads, streets,

01:27:41.591 --> 01:27:44.015
infrastruc water infrastructure, sewer,

01:27:45.347 --> 01:27:49.327
those types of things. In addition to that, almost eighteen million

01:27:49.827 --> 01:27:52.795
dollars was spent on. projects within your city,

01:27:53.309 --> 01:27:57.210
capital asset projects. So I think that's worthy

01:27:57.243 --> 01:28:00.823
to note that those funds were contributed by outside parties

01:28:01.257 --> 01:28:04.853
and not necessarily spent by the city. And then the other was spent

01:28:05.353 --> 01:28:08.787
by the city and for capital improvements. That's really kind

01:28:09.287 --> 01:28:13.492
of a quick review of what we did as auditors, our conclusions, and happy

01:28:13.992 --> 01:28:14.359
to answer any questions.

01:28:20.621 --> 01:28:24.026
The one, uh, the one, uh, statement that you said you felt

01:28:24.526 --> 01:28:27.123
like that, uh, we need to have an asset management, uh,

01:28:28.435 --> 01:28:31.815
software— what have you had good experiences with? We'd love to hear some suggestions of

01:28:32.315 --> 01:28:35.681
what you think is working in other places. Well, I think you've got, um,

01:28:36.052 --> 01:28:39.617
inside of your own system, uh, Springbrook, which is

01:28:40.117 --> 01:28:43.247
your accounting system now, you do have that ability. It's a new module and

01:28:43.747 --> 01:28:47.328
it may have to be an upgrade. But your current software does have the ability

01:28:47.361 --> 01:28:49.125
to do that, so that's where I would look first.

01:28:50.472 --> 01:28:54.242
There's other ones out there. Most of them that I have seen are connected

