01:58:42.616 --> 01:58:46.050
I recently spoke this week, coincidentally, just for something totally unrelated from

01:58:46.066 --> 01:58:49.644
my job, to Robert Bergman. He has been working in AI

01:58:49.789 --> 01:58:52.709
since the '60s. I didn't know it existed in the '60s, but it did.

01:58:53.143 --> 01:58:56.721
He worked for Intel. He's got lots of degrees. He's really smart. He said

01:58:56.753 --> 01:59:00.173
to me that data centers are going to be obsolete really soon, and cities

01:59:00.221 --> 01:59:02.613
are going to be stuck with these big empty buildings that won't be getting used

01:59:03.113 --> 01:59:06.706
because the technology won't— it won't be necessary anymore. And he

01:59:07.206 --> 01:59:09.997
wrote an article called Systems View of AI Governance,

01:59:10.623 --> 01:59:13.865
and he talked about how the laws are not going to catch up with the

01:59:14.365 --> 01:59:16.803
technology. And by the time we realize we need the laws, something bad will have

01:59:17.303 --> 01:59:20.334
happened with the technology. He talked about the energy bottleneck.

01:59:20.896 --> 01:59:25.006
The major AI companies have responded by becoming energy companies of sort. They're contracting

01:59:25.506 --> 01:59:28.907
for dedicated gas plants, batteries, And even nuclear reactors. That buys

01:59:29.003 --> 01:59:32.279
capacity, but on the timescale of power infrastructure, which is measured in years,

01:59:32.311 --> 01:59:35.699
not product cycle. He said the mathematics

01:59:35.763 --> 01:59:39.103
of scaling is unforgiving. Each doubling of compute yields a smaller improvement

01:59:39.135 --> 01:59:42.507
than the last. Training runs for frontier models are projected to cost

01:59:43.007 --> 01:59:46.569
several billion dollars each, and those costs must eventually be recovered— 30 seconds remaining.

01:59:46.906 --> 01:59:50.792
