to come forward just to be closer and ready.

02:37:17.987 --> 02:37:21.134
Mayor, council, thank you for your time.

02:37:21.471 --> 02:37:25.068
Um, we have a brief presentation put together by Russell Sprague to

02:37:25.084 --> 02:37:28.392
talk about GMP 2, and we're seeking the approval of

02:37:28.892 --> 02:37:29.114
the bid award.

02:38:10.830 --> 02:38:14.620
Good. Thank you, Mayor and Council and the

02:38:15.120 --> 02:38:18.380
citizens at large. I'm Russell Sprague with A2S Engineers.

02:38:18.780 --> 02:38:23.020
We are the engineers designing the wastewater expansion.

02:38:23.960 --> 02:38:27.320
Uh, with the 2nd work package on

02:38:27.820 --> 02:38:31.240
the agenda tonight, um, Council has requested

02:38:31.740 --> 02:38:33.180
that we give an overview of the project.

02:38:35.706 --> 02:38:38.891
And kind of where we're headed and what we've done

02:38:39.391 --> 02:38:43.271
to date. So many might not know that the city is broken

02:38:43.771 --> 02:38:46.876
up into 2 service areas. The line here represents the north and the south service

02:38:46.972 --> 02:38:50.156
area. The north area goes to a different

02:38:50.804 --> 02:38:54.466
facility, but the south area goes to Eagle Mountain's wastewater facility.

02:38:54.515 --> 02:38:55.925
You can see a picture of it there.

02:38:58.188 --> 02:39:02.046
Eagle Mountain's growing at a rapid pace. I think everyone knows. One of the fastest

02:39:02.078 --> 02:39:05.020
growing cities in Utah and the entire United States.

02:39:05.812 --> 02:39:09.352
There's been 16 to 21% growth rates just in

02:39:09.852 --> 02:39:13.856
the South Service Area in the last 2 years. It's been estimated Eagle Mountain's responsible

02:39:13.888 --> 02:39:17.545
for at least 10% of the state's growth. The growth

02:39:18.045 --> 02:39:21.174
is straining the wastewater infrastructure, and with that, if there's an increased

02:39:21.674 --> 02:39:25.512
risk of breaking the permit established by Utah Department of Environmental

02:39:26.012 --> 02:39:29.842
Quality, Each household contributes between 250 and

02:39:30.342 --> 02:39:33.455
400 gallons per day into the wastewater plant. So you can imagine

02:39:35.691 --> 02:39:37.131
that adds up pretty fast.

02:39:39.740 --> 02:39:44.280
The planned available wastewater treatment

02:39:44.780 --> 02:39:48.403
over time with this beige line here represents a 16% growth rate,

02:39:48.451 --> 02:39:51.281
which is what we've been seeing the last 2 years at a minimum.

02:39:52.969 --> 02:39:56.405
With exponential growth, it's hard to project that over more than

02:39:56.437 --> 02:39:59.103
10 to 15 years. Um,

02:39:59.809 --> 02:40:03.310
what we're saying is we probably won't see 16% to 20%

02:40:03.422 --> 02:40:06.698
every single year. Yeah. But in the near future,

02:40:06.714 --> 02:40:08.689
that is definitely a possibility.

02:40:09.909 --> 02:40:12.944
Um, so right now, the treatment plant has

02:40:12.976 --> 02:40:16.123
a capacity of right under 2 million gallons per day.

02:40:17.697 --> 02:40:22.882
And this first expansion is going to bring

02:40:23.027 --> 02:40:26.731
the capacity up to 8 million gallons per day. So when we

02:40:26.780 --> 02:40:30.193
talk about wastewater projects, we are quadrupling the size of

02:40:30.693 --> 02:40:34.230
this plant to maybe catch up to the growth

02:40:34.246 --> 02:40:37.588
that we're seeing. And then in

02:40:37.605 --> 02:40:42.174
the near future. So just an overview

02:40:42.255 --> 02:40:46.594
of wastewater comes in dirty. Headworks is the first filter.

02:40:46.723 --> 02:40:49.868
That's where the screens remove grit,

02:40:50.352 --> 02:40:54.361
large objects. I always like to joke, your kids' underpants,

02:40:54.378 --> 02:40:57.465
they flush down the toilet. That's where that gets captured and taken out.

02:41:00.367 --> 02:41:03.865
The biological nutrient removal is what I call a fancy bubble bath for

02:41:04.365 --> 02:41:08.683
bacteria. It's big tanks where good bacteria eat bad

02:41:09.183 --> 02:41:12.728
bacteria and remove nitrogen, phosphorus. Which is

02:41:12.841 --> 02:41:16.562
what— here on the right, it's regulated by the state of Utah. Utah sets strict

02:41:17.062 --> 02:41:19.119
limits on what's allowed in the water we return to the environment.

02:41:20.170 --> 02:41:21.683
Total nitrogen, total phosphorus,

02:41:23.503 --> 02:41:26.740
biochemical oxygen demand, suspended solids, and there's a few others,

02:41:26.885 --> 02:41:30.339
but those are the main ones. And the membrane that we're

02:41:30.839 --> 02:41:34.017
putting in— water flows through these tiny membranes, and they act

02:41:34.517 --> 02:41:37.128
as a superfine strainer, so only clean water can pass through.

02:41:38.687 --> 02:41:42.321
Solids are separated throughout that, and they are dewatered

02:41:42.386 --> 02:41:46.824
and hauled to a landfill. So that's kind of the overview

02:41:47.324 --> 02:41:51.224
of what wastewater treatment does. So right here in

02:41:51.274 --> 02:41:54.437
green is the existing plant. So you have your oxidation ditch here,

02:41:56.316 --> 02:41:59.456
and the proposed expansion is on the west side. Here we

02:41:59.956 --> 02:42:03.931
have a new garage, a new headworks building, the biological nutrient removal

02:42:04.302 --> 02:42:07.799
basins, the membrane building. An electrical and chemical building

02:42:07.912 --> 02:42:10.971
and a solids building. So it's, you know,

02:42:10.987 --> 02:42:14.369
4 buildings and then 1 very,

02:42:14.450 --> 02:42:18.384
very, very large basin that we're planning

02:42:18.497 --> 02:42:19.351
on building.

02:42:22.530 --> 02:42:26.557
So we've gone to the construction manager general contractor

02:42:26.605 --> 02:42:30.374
route of delivering this project, which is we've

02:42:30.390 --> 02:42:33.218
hired a general contractor in the design phase.

02:42:34.274 --> 02:42:37.029
to guarantee us what the maximum price will be.

02:42:37.737 --> 02:42:40.701
So if you hard bid something, when you get bids in, you go to the

02:42:41.201 --> 02:42:45.072
low bid, and that's the bid. This delivery method, you negotiate upfront

02:42:45.572 --> 02:42:48.785
with the contractor what the price will be for the desired scope.

02:42:49.769 --> 02:42:53.495
This is beneficial in a lot of ways. You know

02:42:53.995 --> 02:42:57.430
the price upfront. Work can begin while design is still happening.

02:42:57.560 --> 02:43:01.026
So that's kind of the phased approach we've been talking about. And it

02:43:01.526 --> 02:43:04.003
improves schedule certainty because we can phase it in a certain way.

02:43:05.792 --> 02:43:09.134
The schedule is kind of at lesser

02:43:09.634 --> 02:43:13.062
risk of slipping. So we call it guaranteed maximum

02:43:13.500 --> 02:43:17.138
price. The way we've structured it, it's also work authorization.

02:43:17.235 --> 02:43:20.914
Those 2 are interchangeable. The advantages,

02:43:21.413 --> 02:43:24.022
like I said, early procurement of critical equipment,

02:43:24.634 --> 02:43:28.578
flexibility to address evolving project needs, reduced schedule risk,

02:43:28.595 --> 02:43:32.572
and easier cost control. So we've identified 6 packages

02:43:34.137 --> 02:43:37.768
in this project to accelerate the schedule.

02:43:37.865 --> 02:43:41.745
Essentially, GMP 1, we've already approved back

02:43:41.793 --> 02:43:43.636
in April. That was the membrane filtration equipment.

02:43:46.143 --> 02:43:49.919
GMP 2 is the one on the agenda tonight, and it's the oxidation

02:43:50.419 --> 02:43:53.905
ditch capacity and optimization. We're installing

02:43:54.178 --> 02:43:57.600
pumps and pipes. SCADA equipment to

02:43:58.100 --> 02:44:00.549
increase the oxidation ditch capacity.

02:44:02.390 --> 02:44:05.787
GMP 3 is earthwork. GMP 4 is the bulk MBR

02:44:06.287 --> 02:44:08.330
expansion. Most of the facilities are in GMP 4.

02:44:09.506 --> 02:44:12.776
GMP 5 is the administration building, and GMP 6 is

02:44:12.953 --> 02:44:16.520
kind of commissioning. You do asphalt,

02:44:16.600 --> 02:44:20.199
you do the garage, and kind of the effluent disposal strategy

02:44:20.780 --> 02:44:24.113
at the very end. So you can see the— I think I

02:44:24.613 --> 02:44:27.673
have a schedule here. Right, we've already completed

02:44:28.173 --> 02:44:30.469
GMP 1. That was awarded in April,

02:44:31.454 --> 02:44:35.121
and you can see all of these upgrades are

02:44:36.216 --> 02:44:39.839
planned to be finished early 2029 for startup

02:44:40.660 --> 02:44:43.204
with a year warranty for everything.

02:44:45.890 --> 02:44:50.190
How is expansion funded? Impact fees. So new growth, new connections

02:44:50.688 --> 02:44:54.124
pay the majority of it. Survey payers and also city funds

02:44:54.381 --> 02:44:57.287
pay for the expansion. A little bit.

02:44:57.544 --> 02:45:01.093
So that's kind of an overview of the entire project. GMP 2

02:45:01.285 --> 02:45:04.673
on the agenda tonight. Kind of just a

02:45:04.738 --> 02:45:08.013
little bit background of that. GMP 4 has

02:45:08.061 --> 02:45:11.835
6 million gallons additional capacity scheduled to be online in 2029.

02:45:12.942 --> 02:45:16.852
The current system. So when you look at those projections of influent

02:45:17.352 --> 02:45:20.682
coming into the plant, the current system does not have enough capacity to

02:45:20.698 --> 02:45:23.935
treat the projected 2029 flows, if that

02:45:24.435 --> 02:45:28.085
makes sense. So GMP2 will install pumps and pipes

02:45:28.585 --> 02:45:31.963
and other site improvements that improve that plant performance,

02:45:32.171 --> 02:45:37.508
basically squeezing every drop we can get out of the existing facility

02:45:37.620 --> 02:45:41.685
before the 6 million gallon additional capacity comes online.

02:45:45.207 --> 02:45:48.035
So, this was a— I think this was actually out of order.

02:45:48.533 --> 02:45:53.097
So, plant capacity snapshot is the existing

02:45:53.161 --> 02:45:56.773
plant has a 2.4 million gallon total hydraulic capacity,

02:45:56.966 --> 02:45:59.743
but the loading capacity is actually over 100%.

02:45:59.937 --> 02:46:03.053
And that's an interesting distinction that sometimes is lost.

02:46:05.094 --> 02:46:09.816
Eagle Mountain's wastewater is highly concentrated due

02:46:10.316 --> 02:46:13.501
to everything is new since 1995 or 1996, right?

02:46:14.274 --> 02:46:17.526
So about 1/3 of wastewater treated in the United States

02:46:17.639 --> 02:46:21.230
is rainwater infiltrating into the sewer system. You guys don't have

02:46:21.730 --> 02:46:25.538
that problem because your sewer is so new. So it's an interesting

02:46:25.977 --> 02:46:29.954
caveat. So your influent

02:46:30.454 --> 02:46:33.645
is very, very concentrated with What we call nutrient loading.

02:46:34.864 --> 02:46:38.282
The good bacteria wants to take out nitrogen and phosphorus,

02:46:38.892 --> 02:46:42.182
and they can only do that at a certain rate. So the way

02:46:42.682 --> 02:46:46.371
I like to have an analogy for it is it's like a kitchen table.

02:46:46.435 --> 02:46:50.415
If you have 10 seats, the flow coming in

02:46:50.511 --> 02:46:54.106
is like how many people can sit at the table, and the loading

02:46:54.202 --> 02:46:57.974
is how much food you can provide the guests. If you only have

02:46:58.474 --> 02:47:02.068
half of people there, but everyone's eating a lot of food, the kitchen might run

02:47:02.568 --> 02:47:06.323
out of food before you can seat the guests, if that makes sense. So,

02:47:07.302 --> 02:47:10.786
I don't know how— Right. So that's why nitrogen

02:47:11.286 --> 02:47:14.447
removal suffers even when flow looks acceptable. So right now we're not quite at the

02:47:14.947 --> 02:47:18.253
flow levels of the plant. So GMP2 kind of bridges that

02:47:18.365 --> 02:47:21.882
gap. Today we have, you know, the current capacity is

02:47:22.155 --> 02:47:25.894
less than 2029 demand. So, GMP 2

02:47:25.911 --> 02:47:29.370
bridges the gap into the expansion.

02:47:30.586 --> 02:47:34.089
Also noted is that GMP 2 is part of the master

02:47:34.589 --> 02:47:38.962
plan to have 14 million gallons daily

02:47:39.462 --> 02:47:42.628
flow at the entire plant

02:47:43.128 --> 02:47:46.659
once phase 2 of the MBR expansion is online.

02:47:47.974 --> 02:47:49.782
But that's in the future.

02:47:52.387 --> 02:47:55.884
So the packet contents, we have the work authorization, which is the mechanism to

02:47:55.900 --> 02:47:59.974
authorize work to the contractor we've already selected. It has

02:48:00.006 --> 02:48:03.215
the drawings and specifications, which are quite lengthy, but that's

02:48:03.359 --> 02:48:06.920
all of the— that's how the contractor knows what to build.

02:48:07.144 --> 02:48:10.689
The GMP form is how the dollars are allocated. There's also a work

02:48:10.738 --> 02:48:15.004
directive which tells the contractor how changes in

02:48:15.504 --> 02:48:19.016
the design are to be addressed in the construction phase. And so

02:48:19.516 --> 02:48:23.512
this was just the breakdown, and I think Councilmember Wood alluded

02:48:24.012 --> 02:48:27.686
to this. This is a $4 million project, and this

02:48:28.186 --> 02:48:31.106
is just to bridge the gap until 2029.

02:48:32.439 --> 02:48:35.747
So it's, you know, $2.5 million for the improvements

02:48:36.036 --> 02:48:39.552
and lots of other things in here. We do have

02:48:40.052 --> 02:48:43.550
$1 million in here for excavation, which is

02:48:43.663 --> 02:48:45.718
kind of jumpstarting the next phases.

02:48:47.902 --> 02:48:49.882
So we can, you know, keep the schedule.

02:48:51.924 --> 02:48:55.993
So, so our recommendation is to approve GMP-2. Addresses the near-term capacity gap

02:48:56.493 --> 02:49:00.632
of the plant, maintains reliable plant operations through early 2029

02:49:00.729 --> 02:49:06.025
when GMP-3 through 6 is on schedule to

02:49:06.074 --> 02:49:09.646
be completed. GMP— whoops,

02:49:10.115 --> 02:49:15.045
GMP-3. Oh, sorry. Through GMP-6 remains

02:49:15.077 --> 02:49:17.551
on schedule with this early excavation included in GMP-2.

02:49:18.699 --> 02:49:22.484
And GMP-2 delivers the plant capacity in line with the long-term objectives of

02:49:22.597 --> 02:49:24.361
the, of the plant.

02:49:25.910 --> 02:49:28.278
So that was everything. And if you have any questions.

02:49:30.970 --> 02:49:34.463
Thanks. I appreciate it. I asked for them to explain this while you guys

02:49:34.561 --> 02:49:38.431
were all here. I think it's important for you to understand. the whys.

02:49:39.328 --> 02:49:42.438
Uh, when we're spending $4 million, you ought to know why and where we're spending

02:49:42.454 --> 02:49:45.820
it. Um, I think the important thing that he said there

02:49:46.320 --> 02:49:50.469
is basically we don't have capacity to get through 2029. And so

02:49:50.549 --> 02:49:54.797
while we're making some of these decisions— thank you very much for your presentation— it's

02:49:55.297 --> 02:49:57.987
important for you to understand what some of the urgency of some of these things

02:49:58.019 --> 02:50:01.449
are, why we're forced to make some of these decisions in a,

02:50:01.530 --> 02:50:05.338
in a faster tempo than maybe we would like to. But this is one of

02:50:05.838 --> 02:50:09.740
those reasons. So, thank you. All right, thanks for

02:50:10.111 --> 02:50:13.562
the context. If there's not any questions specifically,

02:50:13.659 --> 02:50:16.869
I'm happy to entertain a motion. Mayor, I'll make the motion.

02:50:17.449 --> 02:50:20.640
I move that the City Council approve a resolution of Eagle Mountain City,

02:50:20.656 --> 02:50:24.057
Utah, awarding the bid for EMC 6.0

02:50:24.154 --> 02:50:26.698
MGD wastewater treatment facility expansion,

02:50:27.801 --> 02:50:31.076
GMP-2 oxidation ditch, and miscellaneous

02:50:31.125 --> 02:50:34.578
site improvements to MGC Contractors, Inc., and approving the contract.

02:50:34.840 --> 02:50:38.786
Second. Okay, we have a motion by Council Member

02:50:39.286 --> 02:50:41.610
Wood and a second by Council Member Clark, and we'll start the vote with,

02:50:41.754 --> 02:50:45.140
uh, Council Member Wright. Yes. Council Member Whiting. Yes. Council Member

02:50:45.640 --> 02:50:48.782
