04:21:10.892 --> 04:21:12.087
State your name for the record, please.

04:21:17.720 --> 04:21:18.177
Daniel Laub.

04:21:22.128 --> 04:21:26.181
Um, hate to be here. Hate to try to address these things. It's no fun,

04:21:26.599 --> 04:21:29.590
obviously, and I, I sympathize with the job you guys are trying to do.

04:21:30.279 --> 04:21:33.534
Um, we have no idea, obviously, what that side of it is like.

04:21:33.927 --> 04:21:37.210
Um, so I'm gonna admit that I

04:21:38.537 --> 04:21:42.419
am pretty much against the tax increases right now, just to kind of state

04:21:42.919 --> 04:21:47.468
my position in the beginning here. Um, the transparency

04:21:47.565 --> 04:21:51.341
just isn't there. We're not seeing— maybe we're just late

04:21:51.357 --> 04:21:55.523
to the game, but it's, it's very hard to see and justify these

04:21:55.556 --> 04:21:58.712
increases, especially the $220 million. And of course, you backed off of that,

04:21:59.278 --> 04:22:02.892
but it leaves us wondering why. Why? that was proposed in the first place,

04:22:03.069 --> 04:22:06.291
why that massive amount of an increase was even proposed.

04:22:07.237 --> 04:22:10.908
Um, and the whole process for me has

04:22:11.004 --> 04:22:14.611
left a lot of distrust in what the process— what

04:22:15.111 --> 04:22:17.625
we're actually even doing. We have no idea what you guys are actually trying to

04:22:17.641 --> 04:22:21.023
accomplish. Um, there's a lot of vague talk about,

04:22:21.039 --> 04:22:24.486
well, roads and improvements and stop signs

04:22:24.986 --> 04:22:28.383
and, you know, a lot of stuff that's just really vague. There's no real numbers

04:22:28.496 --> 04:22:31.354
to anything of what we're going to do and why we're going to do it

04:22:31.386 --> 04:22:34.806
and why it's needed. And right now, with the economy

04:22:34.838 --> 04:22:38.178
the way it is, with— I mean, we're facing all kinds of difficulties.

04:22:39.479 --> 04:22:43.156
Things aren't getting better if the war doesn't stop. We're facing

04:22:43.188 --> 04:22:46.495
a lot more. It could get a lot worse from here. Right now is

04:22:46.560 --> 04:22:49.610
a terrible time to raise taxes. Uh,

04:22:49.931 --> 04:22:53.110
we are grateful for the sheriffs, what they do. And,

04:22:53.446 --> 04:22:57.292
and we would love to try to, to do something maybe

04:22:57.792 --> 04:23:01.362
in the future. For me right now, I don't think it's— we just can't swing

04:23:01.458 --> 04:23:04.759
it. It's just a bad time to be raising costs above where

04:23:05.259 --> 04:23:08.333
we already are. Young people coming in trying to buy homes, I mean,

04:23:08.365 --> 04:23:11.585
that's just completely out of reach with the prices the way they are.

04:23:12.354 --> 04:23:16.200
Add a couple hundred bucks a year to their overall

04:23:16.296 --> 04:23:19.533
cost, and it's, it's, it's enough to put people out of the

04:23:19.824 --> 04:23:20.903
the ability to do it.

04:23:25.279 --> 04:23:30.105
Um, I, I feel— and, and I'm— and I— how

04:23:30.605 --> 04:23:34.141
do I say this? I honestly feel there's a lot of— because

04:23:34.641 --> 04:23:38.200
of lack of transparency, I feel like that we're being lied to.

04:23:38.976 --> 04:23:42.307
I don't understand where you guys are coming from. This— none of this process has

04:23:42.807 --> 04:23:45.986
made sense in like what the needs are. Um, the sheriff thing,

04:23:46.179 --> 04:23:49.305
that kind of makes sense. We kind of understand those numbers. We're, we're getting that.

04:23:50.159 --> 04:23:52.685
Um, I still don't want to do that right now. Um,

04:23:53.398 --> 04:23:56.572
I'd like to say the one thing that I

04:23:57.072 --> 04:24:00.215
thought last time we were here that went well was Jared made sure that everyone

04:24:00.715 --> 04:24:03.886
that wanted to speak had an opportunity to speak, and I'll congratulate him on that.

04:24:04.956 --> 04:24:10.372
And that's all I have to say. Thank you. All right,

04:24:10.775 --> 04:24:13.612
I think we've covered it. I do want to make note that the last 2

04:24:13.983 --> 04:24:17.750
comments for Dale and Daniel, we do have their papers. They were in my stack

04:24:18.250 --> 04:24:21.196
for public hearing. So those finalized. Thank you. Thank you.

04:24:22.020 --> 04:24:25.171
Um, if you spoke and did not fill out a paper, that helps us

04:24:25.671 --> 04:24:27.782
with our record keeping, if you would please. Um,

04:24:28.683 --> 04:24:32.240
seeing no one else for, for our public comment period, I will

04:24:32.932 --> 04:24:34.267
now close our public comment.

04:24:37.162 --> 04:24:40.427
Um, we do have a lot to talk about. If it's okay

04:24:40.879 --> 04:24:43.530
with the council, I can address some of those things that are kind of more

04:24:44.030 --> 04:24:46.623
matter of fact if you guys want to speak. Or we can get right into

04:24:47.123 --> 04:24:51.265
that discussion. Just really quick, one of the things that I hated about Truth

04:24:51.765 --> 04:24:55.521
in Taxation a couple weeks ago was that the residents

04:24:56.021 --> 04:24:59.344
are like, you're not being transparent, we want answers, all of those things.

04:24:59.456 --> 04:25:03.247
And because of the way that public hearing worked, we couldn't answer their

04:25:03.311 --> 04:25:06.251
questions. So I'm left now saying,

04:25:06.588 --> 04:25:10.218
oh my gosh, I want to answer all your questions. So how do we address

04:25:10.718 --> 04:25:14.208
that, you guys? Because We have answers to all the things that they asked about,

04:25:14.694 --> 04:25:18.062
and I know that it's late, so I'm trying to figure out how you guys

04:25:18.159 --> 04:25:21.262
want us to get you the answers so you're not left going,

04:25:21.449 --> 04:25:25.470
what's going on? I was, uh, I was gonna say that

04:25:25.970 --> 04:25:29.407
that's what I offered. I've only got like 4 or 5 maybe that are

04:25:29.907 --> 04:25:32.027
kind of easier, and then if there's something I missed, I'm happy to have that

04:25:32.075 --> 04:25:35.583
conversation as we go through this process. Just try to keep it brief so

04:25:36.083 --> 04:25:38.834
we can keep going. Can I just say one thing? Because it came up so

04:25:39.334 --> 04:25:43.439
many times. Sure. But the 3%, you guys, there's a property

04:25:43.471 --> 04:25:46.647
tax abatement for senior citizens. If you're 67

04:25:46.695 --> 04:25:51.122
or older, you get, um, 20% off

04:25:51.186 --> 04:25:55.132
of your value. So they discount that. So right now you

04:25:55.180 --> 04:25:58.949
only pay on 55% of your value. If you're 67

04:25:59.449 --> 04:26:03.761
or older, you can get another 20% off of that. So that brings the

04:26:04.261 --> 04:26:07.707
total value of your home that you're paying tax on down to 35%.

04:26:08.332 --> 04:26:12.019
And then there's a dollar amount if you're under a certain income

04:26:12.519 --> 04:26:15.642
level that can get given back to you. We also have that

04:26:15.706 --> 04:26:19.120
in our state for our veterans. I'm going to ask that

04:26:19.168 --> 04:26:23.047
our team on comms help get the links out because

04:26:23.144 --> 04:26:26.430
the deadline to that is September 1st. If you guys need

04:26:26.930 --> 04:26:29.940
help filling this stuff out and nobody can help you at the county,

04:26:30.293 --> 04:26:33.643
call me and I will help you. I don't want anyone to go

04:26:34.143 --> 04:26:38.090
home tonight thinking We're trying to screw over seniors. We know

04:26:38.138 --> 04:26:41.930
that there's a property tax abatement, and clearly that's another thing that we've been really

04:26:41.994 --> 04:26:45.400
crappy at telling people about. So let's get everyone in Eagle

04:26:45.900 --> 04:26:48.420
Mountain who's over 67 on that. Thank you.

04:26:49.721 --> 04:26:53.271
Okay. Couple of real quick answers just to clarify

04:26:53.771 --> 04:26:57.079
some things that are objective, I think, not subjective,

04:26:57.737 --> 04:27:00.918
uh, to some of the comments were out there. Uh, number 1,

04:27:01.512 --> 04:27:05.396
I want to state Right off the bat, that I can tell you

04:27:05.896 --> 04:27:08.958
sitting right here, I have no idea where this is going to land. So anybody

04:27:08.990 --> 04:27:12.391
leaves here and says this was predetermined, I don't even know. Uh,

04:27:13.306 --> 04:27:16.098
I can tell you that a little while ago before we started the public comment,

04:27:16.611 --> 04:27:19.900
that I did hear a 0.0009, and I also heard a

04:27:20.400 --> 04:27:23.751
0.0014. And I suspect that the ones that didn't

04:27:24.251 --> 04:27:27.890
give a number are probably somewhere in the middle. I don't know. Uh, there has

04:27:27.986 --> 04:27:30.987
not been a predetermined outcome, and I'm curious to find out myself.

04:27:31.569 --> 04:27:34.821
Number 2, on that note, and I don't even remember who made these,

04:27:35.111 --> 04:27:38.782
some of them repeated. Somebody said that 0.0009

04:27:38.814 --> 04:27:42.194
is on the table. I'm going to tell you, everything's on the table from

04:27:42.274 --> 04:27:46.102
0 to 0.0017.

04:27:46.440 --> 04:27:48.675
That's the max. It's all on the table.

04:27:50.455 --> 04:27:53.915
Um, I think maybe 0.0015 might be the cap

04:27:54.415 --> 04:27:56.042
from our last, but I think it's all on the table because that's what we

04:27:56.542 --> 04:27:59.886
submitted, right? Just making sure. I don't think it's

04:28:00.386 --> 04:28:02.694
going that way, but I want to clarify that's the truth.

04:28:05.562 --> 04:28:08.486
Uh, one, one thing that I— well, and I want to clear up something there,

04:28:08.777 --> 04:28:12.475
and there was one specific comment that really said a

04:28:12.556 --> 04:28:15.999
lot of things about council's luxuries of being

04:28:16.112 --> 04:28:19.540
up here. Oh my goodness, I would love any one of

04:28:19.588 --> 04:28:23.065
those. None of us have a car, including me. I provide my own car.

04:28:23.522 --> 04:28:26.666
I do get a small stipend that doesn't even cover my fuel, let alone my

04:28:27.166 --> 04:28:30.886
car. Uh, none of these guys have a car, a gas

04:28:31.386 --> 04:28:35.345
card. None of them get any of that. Uh, their stipend doesn't

04:28:35.474 --> 04:28:39.404
cover hardly anything. Uh, there's a little bit, and it wouldn't

04:28:39.904 --> 04:28:43.174
cover their expenses, let alone their time. So I'll let you weigh

04:28:43.255 --> 04:28:46.976
in. Yeah, I just wanted to clarify on the, on the car in the,

04:28:47.105 --> 04:28:50.683
in the budget. It's called a car allowance, but in the Code.

04:28:51.342 --> 04:28:55.966
It's actually a phone, internet, travel allowance.

04:28:56.030 --> 04:28:59.852
It's $200 a month per council member. So I

04:29:00.352 --> 04:29:03.914
just wanted to clarify that, and it's not something you submit for

04:29:04.042 --> 04:29:07.928
a reimbursement. It's just part of the code. So I'm open

04:29:07.976 --> 04:29:11.236
to changing that in the code. I just wanted to make sure that got answered

04:29:11.749 --> 04:29:14.945
as well. As far as lifestyle, it like,

04:29:15.443 --> 04:29:19.618
It's not that great, especially right now. So, uh, it's— we're

04:29:20.118 --> 04:29:23.007
just like you guys. I get up and come and do my service, and that's

04:29:23.136 --> 04:29:26.701
it. Uh, so, so I just wanted to make sure that that was clear,

04:29:26.766 --> 04:29:31.054
that there is none of that. Uh, one thing that I wanted to clarify,

04:29:31.809 --> 04:29:35.599
uh, a lot of people keep saying, why didn't we plan for this? Why didn't

04:29:36.099 --> 04:29:39.454
we put the infrastructure ahead of time? I just want to help you understand,

04:29:39.775 --> 04:29:43.225
because I don't want you To pay now for the guy that

04:29:43.725 --> 04:29:46.817
comes in later for a free ride. Growth has to pay for growth. And if

04:29:46.833 --> 04:29:49.911
we do that now, when you say plan, what you're really saying is pay.

04:29:50.521 --> 04:29:53.263
And I don't want you to pay for the next guy, and it's all there

04:29:53.311 --> 04:29:56.918
when he gets here. So growth needs to pay for growth. And one of

04:29:57.418 --> 04:30:00.767
the ways we do that is through a bond. This sewer plant, we need

04:30:01.267 --> 04:30:04.791
2 million dollars— 2 million gallons today. It's about to overflow.

04:30:05.673 --> 04:30:08.639
But I don't think you all should pay that bond payment for the guys that

04:30:09.139 --> 04:30:12.716
are coming in the future. So we bond for it in order for the next

04:30:13.216 --> 04:30:16.377
guy that comes in and pays that impact fee to go towards that payment.

04:30:16.425 --> 04:30:19.396
And then the next year and the next year, that's why we bond. So,

04:30:19.540 --> 04:30:23.009
uh, not that I'm advocating to vote for bonds, but I'm telling you, it's so

04:30:23.509 --> 04:30:26.316
that the next guy pays and you don't. Uh, just a little secret there.

04:30:27.264 --> 04:30:30.346
The other thing about no increase, all the people

04:30:30.846 --> 04:30:34.232
that said keep it flat or don't increase really, really have to understand

04:30:34.732 --> 04:30:37.813
how taxes work. There has been no movement on our taxes, correct?

04:30:37.861 --> 04:30:40.719
That's what we could all agree. There's been no movement for at least 16 years.

04:30:41.474 --> 04:30:45.311
That's incorrect. And the people that say, well, we're dealing with inflation,

04:30:45.327 --> 04:30:49.068
so are we. We're buying this stuff at the store too. The employees

04:30:49.085 --> 04:30:52.794
that are pushing lawnmowers— a lawnmower costs more today than it did 16 years ago.

04:30:53.420 --> 04:30:56.695
The problem is our tax rate's actually going backwards.

04:30:57.129 --> 04:31:00.388
Not only is it not flat, not only is it not growing, it's going backwards.

04:31:00.902 --> 04:31:04.369
So, 16 years ago, or 10 years ago, the rate—

04:31:04.434 --> 04:31:08.928
and I'm going off memory, so I'm going to forget— was it like 0.0013?

04:31:09.827 --> 04:31:13.069
Nothing has changed. And that rate went down to 0.012,

04:31:13.134 --> 04:31:16.922
down to 0.101, to 0.09, to where it is today, to 0.05.

04:31:17.436 --> 04:31:20.566
It's not that we kept it flat. We allowed it to go backwards.

04:31:21.513 --> 04:31:24.900
And so to say tighten your belt or find

04:31:25.400 --> 04:31:28.881
other money, how? It's continually leaking backwards, yet we're

04:31:29.381 --> 04:31:32.734
trying to go forward with more services. So that's more of an

04:31:33.409 --> 04:31:36.044
educational piece. Not that I'm advocating for one or the other.

04:31:36.462 --> 04:31:39.836
I don't know where this is going to land. I just— that is a

04:31:39.916 --> 04:31:43.621
fact that I'm telling you that that's the way they go.

04:31:43.701 --> 04:31:46.944
Look at— you've got your tax bill right there. If you look

04:31:47.073 --> 04:31:50.283
year over year at the previous one, that rate has continued to go down.

04:31:50.331 --> 04:31:54.307
Is that not true? That is true.

04:31:54.372 --> 04:31:57.804
I paid the same property tax for the last 12 years that I

04:31:57.901 --> 04:32:01.937
lived in my house. So, and, and the rate went down in order

04:32:02.437 --> 04:32:04.838
to keep that, so the inflation went past the rate and you continue to go

04:32:05.338 --> 04:32:08.765
backwards. That's right. It's, it's about $220, I think.

04:32:09.695 --> 04:32:12.933
So, so I want to make sure that people understand

04:32:13.433 --> 04:32:16.395
that it's not a matter of trying to raise taxes. We're trying to stay even,

04:32:17.149 --> 04:32:20.868
to even continue to provide services. I am happy

04:32:21.368 --> 04:32:24.602
to sit down and help you walk through that, to explain that. It's not because

04:32:24.618 --> 04:32:28.179
I advocate for a raise. It's none of that. I want you to understand why

04:32:28.227 --> 04:32:32.608
we're in this situation and how can we correct it and move forward. I also,

04:32:32.640 --> 04:32:35.801
uh, some of the rest of stuff that I've commented on Facebook, but I'm telling

04:32:35.833 --> 04:32:39.267
you, there are better days ahead. We have to bridge

04:32:39.299 --> 04:32:42.492
the gap until those collections come in. We can't go backwards

04:32:42.992 --> 04:32:46.744
and provide the same service with a growing population, with added lane miles, added parks,

04:32:47.145 --> 04:32:50.338
added deputies. There's no physical way that we can do it.

04:32:50.386 --> 04:32:54.093
So, uh, I think that's the bulk of, I think, what I

04:32:54.543 --> 04:32:57.501
felt were the questions that I hope that answered some of those.

04:32:58.325 --> 04:33:01.783
If not, I'm happy to talk to anyone, and I'm happy to

04:33:01.896 --> 04:33:03.350
turn it to you guys if you want to add to those.

04:33:05.667 --> 04:33:10.323
If I could, just to Gary's question on mandating

04:33:10.355 --> 04:33:13.912
future councils, that's something that we're not legally allowed

04:33:14.412 --> 04:33:18.160
to do. For example, there's a couple of council members here when they served in

04:33:18.257 --> 04:33:21.665
their time, they couldn't require or obligate or

04:33:22.165 --> 04:33:25.480
bind future councils to do certain things. And so the code

04:33:25.980 --> 04:33:29.568
that we're putting in place simply calls the councils

04:33:29.632 --> 04:33:33.479
to account where they publicly will say, we're not going to maintain that

04:33:33.979 --> 04:33:36.974
coverage ratio and why. And I think as a, as a citizen,

04:33:37.391 --> 04:33:40.500
that would have been nice to have from my perspective.

04:33:41.094 --> 04:33:44.059
So that's what we're trying to do is create some kind of environment or,

04:33:44.364 --> 04:33:47.762
or more than a culture, something that is in our code that

04:33:47.810 --> 04:33:51.626
will force councils to eliminate that code

04:33:51.690 --> 04:33:55.218
to not be held accountable. So I hope that helps answer that question.

04:33:55.523 --> 04:33:59.131
Thank you. That was a great question, by the way. I want to address the

04:33:59.631 --> 04:34:02.451
question of why did we buy land for City Hall?

04:34:03.766 --> 04:34:08.401
Uh, because the cost of land is increasing substantially

04:34:08.497 --> 04:34:11.801
year over year. Unfortunately, it's something we probably should have done 5 or

04:34:12.301 --> 04:34:15.842
6 years ago because it would have been half what it is now.

04:34:16.454 --> 04:34:20.027
And so to be able to do that in the future, again, we have

04:34:20.527 --> 04:34:22.775
a limited amount of money that, that we have to be able to work with.

04:34:23.359 --> 04:34:25.915
We felt like that, that was a good investment in the future, because then that

04:34:26.415 --> 04:34:29.534
land can be developed when the money is there to build City Hall. So I,

04:34:31.544 --> 04:34:34.687
I know that it seems like that there's a lot of deception.

04:34:35.908 --> 04:34:39.117
And man, I wish, Mr. Lab, there was a way for

04:34:39.134 --> 04:34:41.905
me to undo how you're feeling about all this.

04:34:43.367 --> 04:34:46.283
I also understand where you're at too. Um,

04:34:46.444 --> 04:34:49.793
it's not an intentional thing. I said

04:34:50.293 --> 04:34:52.917
in the very beginning, and I, I think I've said this multiple times from this

04:34:53.417 --> 04:34:56.667
dais, we have to be better at communicating in multiple

04:34:57.167 --> 04:35:00.528
ways. I think we've relied heavily on social media, and I

04:35:00.592 --> 04:35:04.134
think that has been a big mistake. Um, to the point

04:35:04.634 --> 04:35:07.979
with the 3 percenters, most of them are not even on there. So how are

04:35:08.479 --> 04:35:11.862
they even getting that information? And I think that we are relying on

04:35:12.362 --> 04:35:15.951
an algorithm that's constantly pushing us towards content they want us to see rather than

04:35:15.984 --> 04:35:19.217
what we want to see. And we have to find some

04:35:19.298 --> 04:35:22.192
better ways to communicate. I'm going to tell you,

04:35:22.224 --> 04:35:25.935
that is one of the commitments that I have, though, is not

04:35:27.140 --> 04:35:31.302
to get stuck on this topic for too long. But I think the code needs

04:35:31.802 --> 04:35:35.217
to start with what we're doing with our property tax. And maybe one of— that's

04:35:35.717 --> 04:35:38.593
maybe one of the things I want to clarify. For some reason, that message is

04:35:38.689 --> 04:35:42.091
not hitting. So I just want to say this on the record so

04:35:42.155 --> 04:35:46.968
you hear me say it. The intent of the very first 0.0017

04:35:47.305 --> 04:35:51.707
was to cover the entire contract with

04:35:51.997 --> 04:35:55.250
stable income. The only stable income that we have as

04:35:55.299 --> 04:35:58.817
a city is property tax. That's it. And that covers the one

04:35:58.931 --> 04:36:02.926
thing that everybody uses. There isn't a single person in this city

04:36:03.023 --> 04:36:05.278
that does not use public safety to some degree.

04:36:06.823 --> 04:36:09.949
In order for us to fund the entire contract, we're not talking about the

04:36:10.449 --> 04:36:13.989
increase. We're talking about the entire contract. It's almost $10 million.

04:36:15.239 --> 04:36:19.151
What we were looking at is making that stable income from property tax

04:36:19.651 --> 04:36:24.425
to pay for that. What that does is then free up the other sales

04:36:24.457 --> 04:36:27.904
tax revenue that we've been supplementing that with so that we can start to

04:36:28.404 --> 04:36:31.738
do some of the things that you guys are talking about, not just It can't

04:36:31.818 --> 04:36:35.414
all be build a road. Some of it has to be, we can't crack seal

04:36:35.462 --> 04:36:39.300
this. It's time to tear the road up and fix the underlayment

04:36:39.800 --> 04:36:43.554
that's washed away over years and to put proper asphalt on it. We need to

04:36:44.054 --> 04:36:46.573
be able to make some of those decisions, but you can't do that when everything

04:36:46.701 --> 04:36:50.314
is always, you know, so low as far as what our income

04:36:50.378 --> 04:36:53.750
is. And so that was what— that's the reason. The reason that we did that

04:36:54.250 --> 04:36:57.491
in the first instance was for that reason. If we fund all

04:36:57.603 --> 04:37:01.505
of our public safety with our stable property tax that allows our variable

04:37:01.601 --> 04:37:05.193
income from sales tax to be used for projects as we set them

04:37:05.690 --> 04:37:09.538
priority-wise. And to your point again, Mr. Laub, I don't

04:37:10.038 --> 04:37:12.857
think we did a good job of saying, here's the list of projects, there's 400

04:37:13.357 --> 04:37:16.897
of them. And we have to prioritize based on what we actually have.

04:37:17.154 --> 04:37:20.681
That's a year-over-year decision. We'll have to look at that and say,

04:37:20.697 --> 04:37:24.449
look, we only have $6 million. We can't build this,

04:37:24.465 --> 04:37:28.136
this, and this, but we can hit maybe these 3 things in here,

04:37:28.168 --> 04:37:30.621
or maybe we can leverage that money in a way that will allow us to

04:37:31.121 --> 04:37:34.122
do maybe one of the bigger projects. Our other problem is, is that we also

04:37:34.187 --> 04:37:38.006
have to have somebody to do the project. And if you don't have manpower

04:37:38.506 --> 04:37:40.676
to do it, how do you get the project done? I think it was Charlotte

04:37:41.176 --> 04:37:45.083
that said we have projects that have been there year over year. The reason

04:37:45.115 --> 04:37:46.567
why? Nobody to do them.

04:37:48.444 --> 04:37:52.199
So we're trying to fix some of those problems. Again, I want

04:37:52.699 --> 04:37:55.196
to be just upfront with what the problems are that we're facing. You need to

04:37:55.696 --> 04:37:58.330
know what they are so that we can make better decisions. And I hope you

04:37:58.830 --> 04:38:02.501
guys come back with better ideas. Whoever the gentleman was that said maybe we

04:38:02.662 --> 04:38:06.484
need to raise the fees, we have tried everything we can

04:38:06.516 --> 04:38:10.129
to stave off the state. I'm going to tell you that right now, to keep

04:38:10.629 --> 04:38:13.903
them from dumping every single house that they want to be built in

04:38:14.403 --> 04:38:17.741
the state in Eagle Mountain. And one other thing I want to make with regards

04:38:18.241 --> 04:38:21.531
to the data centers— those data centers prevented them from

04:38:22.031 --> 04:38:25.659
putting houses there. That was their intent from the state. They wanted to fill

04:38:25.771 --> 04:38:29.818
every single square mile. of Eagle Mountain with houses. That enabled

04:38:30.318 --> 04:38:33.366
us— not only do we get a benefit, by the way, from that, but it

04:38:33.866 --> 04:38:36.770
stopped them from being able to be houses that we can't— we cannot service them.

04:38:37.043 --> 04:38:41.041
I mean, that's the truth. We're stretched. We have— we had 2

04:38:41.541 --> 04:38:45.039
cities that were built at the same time with nothing in between, and we were

04:38:45.539 --> 04:38:49.535
trying to connect everything with services, with sewer plants, with trunk

04:38:50.035 --> 04:38:53.629
lines, with all of those things with no money. And I just—

04:38:54.129 --> 04:38:56.455
that's the truth. That's the truth I want you to be aware of that we're

04:38:56.955 --> 04:39:00.614
trying to solve now, you know, 10, 15 years later. So I appreciate the

04:39:01.114 --> 04:39:04.130
comments. Hopefully that gives a little bit of context to some of those things.

04:39:05.350 --> 04:39:08.465
Anybody wants to have a conversation, my telephone number is

04:39:08.722 --> 04:39:12.142
always there. You can call me. I may not be able to answer that.

04:39:12.351 --> 04:39:15.642
My job has become a little more involved during the day, but I will definitely

04:39:16.142 --> 04:39:18.864
call you back. Anybody who's called me knows that that's the case. So. Reach out

04:39:19.364 --> 04:39:23.396
to me. So I

04:39:23.412 --> 04:39:26.801
wanted to address— you had a question about the 183%. And so,

04:39:26.882 --> 04:39:29.902
and I'm happy to stay after and talk if you're able to stay, and we

04:39:30.402 --> 04:39:33.536
can talk through that. But I'll explain briefly. I'm the one that came up

04:39:34.036 --> 04:39:37.417
with that number. That was also from last— the Truth in Taxation. There was no

04:39:37.917 --> 04:39:41.330
predetermined number at that time. I was the one that proposed that to the council.

04:39:41.524 --> 04:39:44.732
The council at the time supported it. We've now repealed it. So that number doesn't

04:39:45.232 --> 04:39:48.522
exist anymore. But, but the— here's, here's the math I used to come up with

04:39:49.022 --> 04:39:52.499
that number. So, understanding that what Councilmember Wood just

04:39:52.999 --> 04:39:56.524
said, that the intent of the tax increase was to place the property

04:39:56.556 --> 04:40:00.341
tax dollars and public safety together, that would cover the new growth in public

04:40:00.841 --> 04:40:04.542
safety. And then sales tax dollars were previously supporting public safety,

04:40:04.655 --> 04:40:07.734
would then be able to divert to other, to other resources.

04:40:07.782 --> 04:40:11.919
So, so the 183% would clear our

04:40:12.272 --> 04:40:16.399
our new hires that we needed for the Sheriff's Department. It would also hire

04:40:16.848 --> 04:40:19.706
a minimum number of employees that we need to fill some staffing needs,

04:40:20.284 --> 04:40:23.576
which that was a pared-down list, by the way. It's public. It's a public list.

04:40:23.672 --> 04:40:26.547
Anybody can see exactly what that list was.

04:40:26.803 --> 04:40:29.999
We debated for a long time coming up with those. So it gave us the

04:40:30.499 --> 04:40:34.430
sheriffs, gave us that, that the minimum number of employees, and then

04:40:34.930 --> 04:40:38.891
it gave a small wage increase as well. Our city employees

04:40:38.939 --> 04:40:43.202
are already paid somewhere between 80 to 85% of the median for

04:40:43.474 --> 04:40:47.143
equivalent municipal work. So the wage

04:40:47.191 --> 04:40:50.316
increase doesn't even get them up to 100%, but gets them up for a little

04:40:50.816 --> 04:40:54.995
bit, and then it leaves a little bit more somewhere in the $2 million-ish range

04:40:55.668 --> 04:40:59.145
that could then be used toward accelerating capital projects within

04:40:59.834 --> 04:41:03.327
our city. But for me, what that also

04:41:03.343 --> 04:41:06.437
then did, that $2 million, capital projects now,

04:41:07.288 --> 04:41:10.468
but what it allows for 20, you know, next year when we come

04:41:10.516 --> 04:41:14.032
into this cycle, we know that given the population increase, the sheriffs will need

04:41:14.532 --> 04:41:17.886
more. So, in my mind, that was budgeting a little bit extra to

04:41:18.386 --> 04:41:22.221
absorb a potential next year increase. So that's how I came up with 183.

04:41:22.382 --> 04:41:25.882
That's why I proposed that to the council. And that's where we landed

04:41:25.978 --> 04:41:29.591
on that. But if you want to talk more about that afterwards, I'm super

04:41:30.091 --> 04:41:33.461
happy to meet with you. Um, and then I'll just address some other things real

04:41:33.525 --> 04:41:37.299
quick. So, uh, a citizen commented, and I,

04:41:37.379 --> 04:41:40.864
I should have written down everybody's name, and I apologize. Separate tax rate for businesses.

04:41:40.912 --> 04:41:44.075
It's an excellent idea that needs to happen at the state level.

04:41:44.766 --> 04:41:48.025
Um, yeah, yeah, go ahead. I'm going to jump in and again

04:41:48.138 --> 04:41:51.414
throw a bone to one of our data center partners, and I don't mind saying

04:41:51.446 --> 04:41:54.850
which one. In this case, QTS. Uh, not only did

04:41:54.898 --> 04:41:58.736
they say, I agree with that, they also reached out to our legislative

04:41:58.752 --> 04:42:01.788
assistant. I don't even know if I've told the council this and said, Will you

04:42:02.288 --> 04:42:06.153
help us write legislation and make that happen? Is that true, Evan? Evan's our legislative

04:42:06.653 --> 04:42:10.227
guy. QTS said, we want that. We want to pay more so

04:42:10.244 --> 04:42:13.390
you pay less. So I want that to be out there for you guys to

04:42:13.890 --> 04:42:17.925
understand what QTS specifically is doing on that piece. They agree

04:42:18.425 --> 04:42:21.539
with you. Okay, and then a resident talked about why don't

04:42:22.039 --> 04:42:25.779
we have volunteers that help the police? We actually do. It's called the VIPS program,

04:42:25.827 --> 04:42:29.766
Volunteers in Police Service. We need more VIPs. So if you're—

04:42:29.783 --> 04:42:33.006
if that's something that interests you, go talk to the VIPs. I'm sure if you

04:42:33.506 --> 04:42:35.419
look online, we'll be able to find their, their contact info.

04:42:37.992 --> 04:42:41.109
Hey, we're recording the meeting, so we're happy to answer that question,

04:42:41.125 --> 04:42:45.624
but nobody can hear you. So yeah,

04:42:45.641 --> 04:42:48.945
so you can talk to our sheriffs after, or they could go out

04:42:49.445 --> 04:42:51.710
in the hall. They've got all the information, and we, we're happy to get that

04:42:51.759 --> 04:42:54.333
to you. I just— we just can't hear their Recording. So, no, I'm sorry.

04:42:54.750 --> 04:42:57.782
And, and I'll tell you, just to back up a little bit on the hires.

04:42:57.959 --> 04:43:01.585
So we had to pare down the original list of, you know,

04:43:01.825 --> 04:43:05.419
requests that came into us. But one that we pared down that we probably

04:43:05.919 --> 04:43:09.253
need to revisit once there's funding for it is a community services director who would

04:43:09.349 --> 04:43:12.622
then be able to help in a number of ways,

04:43:12.815 --> 04:43:16.649
and one including communications, but organizing volunteerism and things

04:43:17.149 --> 04:43:20.033
like that in the city. So, but we do have volunteer police force that helps

04:43:20.533 --> 04:43:24.955
with things that you're talking about. Um, and I have been— anyway,

04:43:25.067 --> 04:43:28.610
and I won't talk a lot about this now, but it's— there's a fundraiser that

04:43:29.110 --> 04:43:30.806
we're going to be putting on for them that I, I'll talk about later in

04:43:30.838 --> 04:43:34.140
the meeting for anybody that stays around later. Uh, but that's there, and it's a—

04:43:34.640 --> 04:43:37.042
that's a really good question. It's something— it's a program that we should, we should

04:43:37.542 --> 04:43:40.616
increase and enhance and enlarge. So, uh,

04:43:41.177 --> 04:43:45.041
I appreciate that. Um, DJ, I really appreciate your comments.

04:43:45.854 --> 04:43:48.965
Thank you. Part of, part of the challenge of this setting

04:43:49.239 --> 04:43:52.592
is being able to, like, we have

04:43:52.624 --> 04:43:56.168
to listen and speak, but we can't dialogue and just go back and

04:43:56.668 --> 04:43:59.096
forth at the same time. And I think when, when you're in a setting when

04:43:59.128 --> 04:44:01.750
you can go back and forth at the same time, it helps to clear up

04:44:02.250 --> 04:44:05.923
a lot of misunderstandings. I can see how you, you took that statement from

04:44:06.423 --> 04:44:09.030
a handful of days ago the way you did, and I apologize if it came

04:44:09.078 --> 04:44:12.780
across incorrectly. Same with Melissa. So I'll

04:44:13.280 --> 04:44:16.880
clarify it a little bit here. So when I was just trying to think of

04:44:17.380 --> 04:44:21.120
an example of when a leader had made a decision that

04:44:21.620 --> 04:44:25.130
the group necessarily didn't necessarily like,

04:44:25.860 --> 04:44:28.700
but he took them to a place where he felt it would be most successful.

04:44:29.080 --> 04:44:32.780
And that was the extent of the analogy. I didn't mean anything relative

04:44:33.280 --> 04:44:36.460
to taxpayers being on this side or that side. It was us as a community

04:44:36.960 --> 04:44:39.520
together, all in the union, not in the Confederate. So I apologize if that was

04:44:40.020 --> 04:44:42.560
that confusing. No, I think that's great. came across incorrectly. And that really was the

04:44:43.060 --> 04:44:46.040
extent of it. I've— it's America 250, so I've been reading a lot of history

04:44:46.540 --> 04:44:48.490
books this year, and that was just the first thing that came to my mind.

04:44:48.500 --> 04:44:51.840
But I really appreciate that you brought that up. And that's what's

04:44:52.340 --> 04:44:55.680
healthy with communication, because it enables whether the speaker or

04:44:56.180 --> 04:44:59.360
the listener to clarify what their intent was. And so it was appropriate for

04:44:59.860 --> 04:45:01.260
you to bring it up in a public setting. It was made in a public

04:45:01.760 --> 04:45:05.420
meeting. So thank you. I appreciate that. And I'll

04:45:05.920 --> 04:45:09.160
clarify. So the reason why I brought up speed tables is because one of the

04:45:09.660 --> 04:45:13.330
other residents at the public hearing Specifically asked council members to name a capital

04:45:13.830 --> 04:45:16.668
project they were interested in. That's the reason we brought up speed tables. It's on

04:45:16.684 --> 04:45:19.926
top of my mind. It's at the top of their minds. Meant nothing beyond that,

04:45:19.974 --> 04:45:23.409
aside from that. And the reason why I brought up the Fauci thing is to

04:45:23.986 --> 04:45:27.662
applaud us together, that we need a healthy skepticism of

04:45:27.694 --> 04:45:30.904
government leadership, and really to applaud the number

04:45:31.404 --> 04:45:34.483
of people that showed up last time and tonight. Like, this is healthy. We need

04:45:34.756 --> 04:45:37.885
to be skeptical of the decisions that government leaders are

04:45:38.385 --> 04:45:41.730
making on all levels. Thank you. So that's really the intent.

04:45:41.760 --> 04:45:43.950
And I'd be happy to talk to you afterwards if there was something additional.

04:45:45.460 --> 04:45:48.360
I'm always happy to talk to you. So thank you for coming. Thank you for

04:45:48.860 --> 04:45:51.940
your comments. I appreciate that. Let's see,

04:45:52.440 --> 04:45:56.060
I think, yeah, I think that's— I think that's— oh,

04:45:56.560 --> 04:46:00.760
I do believe it would be important, I think, to help talk about the

04:46:01.260 --> 04:46:04.520
sort of the state requirements that are placed upon the city and how we communicate

04:46:05.020 --> 04:46:07.580
the tax truth and taxation. I don't know if we want to go into that.

04:46:07.720 --> 04:46:09.920
I think there needs to be work done at the state level, but I'm not

04:46:10.420 --> 04:46:13.799
here to disparage them any more than I want them to disparage us. So I

04:46:14.299 --> 04:46:17.702
prefer that, that we understand if there's questions, read the state code.

04:46:18.457 --> 04:46:21.959
We, we are working with our legislators. We're working on legislation to raise taxes on

04:46:22.007 --> 04:46:24.721
data centers. Can't wait, you know, but, but I'm, uh,

04:46:25.540 --> 04:46:28.737
I'm not in a hurry to— we need to fix the process because it's kind

04:46:29.237 --> 04:46:32.527
of got us handcuffed, if that's fair to say. One thing that came up earlier,

04:46:32.993 --> 04:46:37.056
we talked about how we can make sure our residents know what we're facing

04:46:37.169 --> 04:46:40.622
earlier. And we're talking about, in addition

04:46:41.122 --> 04:46:45.328
to the code that they are working on to kind of lock public safety

04:46:45.344 --> 04:46:48.154
money into its own little space, um,

04:46:48.475 --> 04:46:51.864
creating something so that there's specific metrics that have to be

04:46:52.364 --> 04:46:56.008
given to council every year, but they're also given to the residents. So you guys

