05:19:03.301 --> 05:19:07.020
the conversations with the, with the council about the

05:19:07.520 --> 05:19:11.338
budget process in general. So one of the challenges

05:19:11.675 --> 05:19:14.873
is having been

05:19:14.889 --> 05:19:18.455
involved in a number of meetings, right? We've— you

05:19:18.520 --> 05:19:21.975
guys are all aware of, we have a lot of internal staff meetings,

05:19:22.073 --> 05:19:24.217
we have a lot of council meetings, there's been a lot of work that has

05:19:24.900 --> 05:19:29.155
gone into everything. But what I can recognize is that

05:19:29.572 --> 05:19:32.093
having been involved in all of it, a lot of times when we go back

05:19:32.593 --> 05:19:36.364
and we look at the communication, it makes sense. It makes sense to us

05:19:36.396 --> 05:19:39.543
because we've been a part of it the entire time.

05:19:40.266 --> 05:19:43.638
But having seen it from the citizen standpoint, if you're

05:19:44.138 --> 05:19:47.796
just stepping into it and all you're doing is looking at the, um,

05:19:48.037 --> 05:19:51.231
like the budget and brief or things like that, it doesn't— cover a lot of

05:19:51.263 --> 05:19:54.920
the details that we covered over hours of different meetings.

05:19:55.273 --> 05:19:59.522
When we do our budget process, we do break it down into different work

05:20:00.022 --> 05:20:03.916
meetings where we start to talk about operational costs and personnel and

05:20:04.416 --> 05:20:08.102
what those requests are related to that. We have a separate meeting where we talk

05:20:08.602 --> 05:20:11.389
about our capital improvement plan and those particular projects.

05:20:12.432 --> 05:20:15.687
We have just

05:20:15.880 --> 05:20:19.119
areas where we break it down. And then when we're trying to put it all

05:20:19.247 --> 05:20:22.760
together at the very end, A lot of that detail

05:20:23.017 --> 05:20:26.514
is missed if people didn't attend

05:20:26.547 --> 05:20:30.702
those particular meetings. So we've made note a lot of the feedback and we'll

05:20:30.782 --> 05:20:34.183
work towards improving our processes so that

05:20:34.683 --> 05:20:37.793
at the end, anybody who jumps in regardless

05:20:38.293 --> 05:20:41.628
of the timeline, it's, it's clear to them on what is taking place

05:20:42.128 --> 05:20:45.863
in the budget's changes from year over to

05:20:46.363 --> 05:20:50.262
year from that. One of the complexities that we deal with as

05:20:50.375 --> 05:20:54.469
a city has to do with the

05:20:54.969 --> 05:20:58.981
different activities from a governmental standpoint versus our business-type

05:20:59.481 --> 05:21:02.433
activities, the restrictions in funds. As you guys know,

05:21:02.481 --> 05:21:05.644
there's a lot of resources that come into play. There's been a lot of

05:21:05.692 --> 05:21:09.545
questions about our development and why can't

05:21:10.045 --> 05:21:12.275
we require development to just pay more money.

05:21:13.157 --> 05:21:16.105
One of the things I wanted to point out about that was a number of

05:21:16.201 --> 05:21:19.935
years ago, I think it was 2022, there were

05:21:21.040 --> 05:21:25.175
some new bills passed at the state legislature that

05:21:25.675 --> 05:21:29.998
restricts the fees that we collect related to development-related.

05:21:30.142 --> 05:21:34.420
So when they're paying an application fee or they're paying a building permit,

05:21:35.830 --> 05:21:39.693
those charges are restricted and can only be

05:21:40.193 --> 05:21:43.948
used to provide the service that someone is paying for. That's really

05:21:44.448 --> 05:21:47.930
the difference between a fee and a tax. And so the state was coming down

05:21:48.430 --> 05:21:51.752
and identifying, if we are charging more money than it really

05:21:51.880 --> 05:21:55.397
costs to issue that building permit and provide those inspection services,

05:21:55.991 --> 05:21:59.555
then what we're doing is levying a tax, and we're taking that to help cover

05:21:59.668 --> 05:22:03.441
other operational costs within the general fund. And the state has said you're

05:22:03.941 --> 05:22:07.851
not allowed to do that. With those restrictions that went into place,

05:22:07.883 --> 05:22:11.474
it did impact our ability to fund other

05:22:11.974 --> 05:22:14.407
things that we do consider growth-related, such as,

05:22:15.625 --> 05:22:19.648
you know, the public safety costs, the enhancements to

05:22:20.148 --> 05:22:23.303
parks and things like that that come with, with the growth.

05:22:23.976 --> 05:22:28.112
We're not able to use those resources anymore. That partially

05:22:28.144 --> 05:22:31.734
led to why we decided to pull that out into its own special

05:22:32.234 --> 05:22:35.532
revenue fund. Because those resources are restricted and can

05:22:35.676 --> 05:22:39.026
only be used for that purpose. And it— we

05:22:39.042 --> 05:22:42.343
wanted to make sure it was clear that that money isn't available for

05:22:42.391 --> 05:22:45.820
us to use for day-in and day-out operations for other services

05:22:45.916 --> 05:22:49.538
within the general fund. So that was one of the changes that has taken

05:22:50.038 --> 05:22:53.673
place over the last couple of years that removed some resources

05:22:53.769 --> 05:22:57.679
that the city did have to help pay for those growth-related

05:22:57.711 --> 05:23:00.949
costs. That is not something that's available to

05:23:01.046 --> 05:23:01.728
us anymore.

05:23:05.751 --> 05:23:09.686
There's a lot of talk about just the rate, and people

05:23:10.186 --> 05:23:13.347
like to compare us to like Lehigh or Saratoga Springs.

05:23:14.120 --> 05:23:18.277
So when we talk about, as we've done for a lot, about what the property

05:23:18.293 --> 05:23:19.885
tax rate is,

05:23:21.763 --> 05:23:25.026
the rate is what we're always talking about, but the end result is what we

05:23:25.526 --> 05:23:28.854
need is how much revenue do we need to operate, um, the city?

05:23:29.543 --> 05:23:33.773
So when people say, hey, well, Lehigh City has a rate

05:23:34.273 --> 05:23:39.397
that's much lower than ours, well, the rate that they have in place generates $15.9

05:23:39.897 --> 05:23:43.451
million for them. And so it's not about

05:23:43.951 --> 05:23:47.985
how high our rate is, it's about the revenue that we're generating to provide

05:23:48.033 --> 05:23:51.334
the needs for our community. So it

05:23:51.834 --> 05:23:55.100
does make it hard when we try to compare compare, because there

05:23:55.293 --> 05:23:58.595
are a lot of unique things here in Eagle Mountain that came up tonight,

05:23:58.756 --> 05:24:01.896
um, where people talked about how we have 2 different communities and we have a

05:24:02.396 --> 05:24:05.185
lot of infrastructure that has to connect everything in between.

05:24:06.411 --> 05:24:10.784
And, um, the rate that was originally, um, adopted last,

05:24:11.026 --> 05:24:16.376
um, at the Truth in Taxation hearing of the 0.0015,

05:24:17.948 --> 05:24:21.238
um, although the rate was higher compared

05:24:21.738 --> 05:24:25.079
to Lehigh or Saratoga Springs, the revenue that it generated was less than both of

05:24:25.579 --> 05:24:29.017
those communities would also see. And they also have a significantly

05:24:29.023 --> 05:24:34.131
greater commercial resource available

05:24:34.583 --> 05:24:37.699
where they have higher sales tax dollars that are coming in.

05:24:38.280 --> 05:24:41.913
But it's really about what are the needs of our community. That's really all

05:24:42.413 --> 05:24:45.535
we're looking at. It's not about comparing us to other cities.

05:24:45.584 --> 05:24:48.891
It's about what do we need in our community to meet service levels that

05:24:48.923 --> 05:24:52.448
our residents expect, and to be able to,

05:24:52.705 --> 05:24:54.820
again, always look sustainably.

05:24:55.637 --> 05:25:00.028
And that's our goals and our objectives when

05:25:00.060 --> 05:25:02.431
we come forward and we look at budgeting.

05:25:03.136 --> 05:25:06.886
Some of the additional questions that were raised were

05:25:06.934 --> 05:25:10.283
about, you know, the amount of

05:25:11.116 --> 05:25:14.545
money we had in our capital projects fund already.

05:25:15.427 --> 05:25:19.416
And so again, a couple of years ago, that reserve

05:25:19.448 --> 05:25:23.006
was a lot higher, and it is higher because we had

05:25:23.214 --> 05:25:27.460
proceeds related to our road projects for when

05:25:27.960 --> 05:25:31.065
we had issued our sales tax bond in 2022 to fund

05:25:31.565 --> 05:25:34.238
a bunch of our big road projects that all got completed,

05:25:35.632 --> 05:25:39.317
and those funds have been spent down. So when we see things that

05:25:39.817 --> 05:25:44.047
say fund balance, it doesn't necessarily mean it's money we can There's

05:25:44.547 --> 05:25:48.270
a lot of different resources that tie into that about what that money

05:25:48.302 --> 05:25:49.297
can be used for.

05:25:50.694 --> 05:25:54.243
And it does make it hard on these summary documents.

05:25:54.387 --> 05:25:58.000
Obviously, people— it just looks like a savings account

05:25:58.096 --> 05:26:01.677
with a total amount of money, and why can't we use that? So we

05:26:01.773 --> 05:26:05.145
will look at being able to break that down where we can see the different

05:26:05.645 --> 05:26:08.921
classifications about what has already been committed or assigned. to projects,

05:26:09.001 --> 05:26:12.350
what's restricted specifically for certain types of spending like

05:26:12.399 --> 05:26:15.941
our transportation dollars. So there's definitely some things that we

05:26:16.005 --> 05:26:19.947
can do to try to communicate that information

05:26:20.060 --> 05:26:21.938
better about what is going on.

05:26:23.876 --> 05:26:27.878
I want to actually just jump into some of these items and

05:26:27.927 --> 05:26:32.027
about our budget process and timeline

05:26:32.527 --> 05:26:35.167
and what the goals that we, we had hoped to be able to accomplish.

05:26:35.248 --> 05:26:38.956
So When we do look at our budget and planning,

05:26:39.228 --> 05:26:42.964
the main things that we are really always focusing on is trying

05:26:42.996 --> 05:26:47.693
to align those priorities, plan for the future, and build consistency.

05:26:48.350 --> 05:26:52.101
That really is the basis behind all the things that we're doing

05:26:52.213 --> 05:26:55.869
that we roll up to, to make sure that we are able to,

05:26:56.766 --> 05:27:00.165
again, sustain our community long-term and that it's not just

05:27:00.665 --> 05:27:03.408
a place that people come to because it's the only place they can afford,

05:27:03.906 --> 05:27:07.056
but hopefully it's a place that people come to because Eagle Mountain is a

05:27:07.556 --> 05:27:10.061
community that they want to be a part of.

05:27:12.656 --> 05:27:16.721
With our budget process and the cycles that we have,

05:27:17.235 --> 05:27:21.332
we do identify, and it is just cyclical

05:27:21.832 --> 05:27:25.078
in that way. Our budget process is a year-long process. We do

05:27:25.578 --> 05:27:28.839
start over again in October. October with that,

05:27:28.967 --> 05:27:32.130
but it does involve the planning, the budgeting, the monitoring,

05:27:32.403 --> 05:27:35.085
and the engagement that we have.

05:27:35.791 --> 05:27:39.741
So we did have a number of public

05:27:39.870 --> 05:27:44.366
meetings. We made an effort to attend some of these events

05:27:44.866 --> 05:27:48.107
that the, that the city put on this year and to run a booth.

05:27:48.155 --> 05:27:51.254
We've done that for the last couple years where we actually have a budget booth.

05:27:51.430 --> 05:27:55.157
We're trying to get out to the So we recognize that people can't always make

05:27:55.657 --> 05:27:59.142
it to meetings, and so we've made an effort to try to get out into

05:27:59.642 --> 05:28:03.127
the community places where people might be to try to share and pass

05:28:03.256 --> 05:28:06.710
on the information and invite people. I— this is what I do,

05:28:06.726 --> 05:28:09.442
and I love it. And I know you as a council have heard me say

05:28:09.458 --> 05:28:13.089
it a number of times of how often we have a public hearing

05:28:13.202 --> 05:28:16.480
for budget processes and nobody shows up for it. And,

05:28:16.656 --> 05:28:20.349
um, trying to find a way That people

05:28:20.366 --> 05:28:23.947
are engaged and want to know what's going on, not just because we do

05:28:24.447 --> 05:28:26.693
a tax hearing, but because they,

05:28:27.725 --> 05:28:30.855
they want to be a part of that process all the time.

05:28:31.194 --> 05:28:34.776
And I welcome that, and I'm grateful to see

05:28:35.276 --> 05:28:36.991
so many people be involved at this point.

05:28:39.282 --> 05:28:42.567
When we look at our— the budget in terms of the system of

05:28:43.067 --> 05:28:46.496
a lot of things that are feeding into it, The strategic

05:28:46.560 --> 05:28:50.577
plan is a high level where we're trying to— again,

05:28:50.641 --> 05:28:53.678
this is where the council set that really high long-term vision.

05:28:54.337 --> 05:28:58.338
It is covering typically a 5 to 10 year plan of

05:28:58.595 --> 05:29:02.323
what we're trying to accomplish in the community. And so when we budget, a lot

05:29:02.823 --> 05:29:05.601
of times we talk a lot about line items, and those are things that are

05:29:06.101 --> 05:29:09.923
needed for accounting purposes. But really, all those line items roll up

05:29:10.004 --> 05:29:13.682
into services, and what is the service level that we're providing

05:29:13.794 --> 05:29:16.926
to our community, and how do we

05:29:17.007 --> 05:29:20.396
meet that. And the strategic plan is one of those things that

05:29:20.896 --> 05:29:24.636
help us make sure that when we are enhancing programs or we have requests

05:29:25.136 --> 05:29:29.663
to increase service levels, that it falls in line with what the council is

05:29:29.759 --> 05:29:33.068
trying to accomplish for our community. So we do

05:29:33.132 --> 05:29:36.200
look at the strategic plan. A large portion of

05:29:36.700 --> 05:29:39.990
our budget is our capital infrastructure, and it's not just

05:29:40.490 --> 05:29:44.038
about building the new infrastructure, but it's also about providing the funds

05:29:44.070 --> 05:29:48.583
to maintain that. What we don't want is to require

05:29:48.647 --> 05:29:52.694
all of our developers to put in amazing parks and amenities for

05:29:52.742 --> 05:29:56.035
our residents that then we're not able to maintain, and all it

05:29:56.535 --> 05:30:00.114
is is an asset that is deteriorating. It's important that

05:30:00.614 --> 05:30:03.297
we're able to maintain that quality of life for the residents who live here,

05:30:03.428 --> 05:30:06.612
as well as those residents that will move in into the future.

05:30:11.047 --> 05:30:14.210
Performance measures. So one of the things actually that we've

05:30:14.710 --> 05:30:18.002
talked about with the— I think for the last 2 years we've been talking internally

05:30:18.502 --> 05:30:21.705
and we've been talking a little bit with the council, and that is about

05:30:22.205 --> 05:30:26.422
trying to talk more about the budget from a service

05:30:26.519 --> 05:30:29.643
level standpoint. And with that comes those performance measures. performance measures,

05:30:30.799 --> 05:30:34.701
and then the accounting and the reportability to where we can respond

05:30:34.797 --> 05:30:38.506
and show the council what the result of

05:30:39.006 --> 05:30:42.231
the efforts put forth, and that we can say if we're adding more money to

05:30:42.440 --> 05:30:46.647
provide a service, has that improved our

05:30:46.775 --> 05:30:50.115
ability to meet that end objective? So implementing

05:30:50.163 --> 05:30:54.001
some of those performance measure tactics is something that we have been moving forward with.

05:30:54.326 --> 05:30:56.004
for the last 2 years within our budget.

05:30:59.443 --> 05:31:03.262
The relationship between the budget process and long-term financial planning.

05:31:03.893 --> 05:31:08.131
Again, these are all the items

05:31:08.147 --> 05:31:11.612
that kind of go in and feed into that overall. One of

05:31:12.112 --> 05:31:15.383
the big differences when we're talking with our governmental funds is it really

05:31:15.496 --> 05:31:18.940
is— and when I talk governmental funds, that means that those are the

05:31:19.440 --> 05:31:23.474
funding sources that come really from sales tax dollars. You're providing a service

05:31:23.974 --> 05:31:27.585
that you aren't able to charge a fee for to cover that service. It's a

05:31:28.085 --> 05:31:32.883
service that's a benefit to everyone, and therefore taxes

05:31:32.947 --> 05:31:36.367
are what's used to cover it. And because it's taxes,

05:31:37.266 --> 05:31:40.542
a lot of the, the what you're looking at really

05:31:40.718 --> 05:31:43.609
is designed to be short-term, and that is why the,

05:31:44.171 --> 05:31:47.446
the state has put a maximum

05:31:47.946 --> 05:31:50.254
on the 35% fund balances, they want you to only raise the money that you

05:31:50.754 --> 05:31:53.454
need for this year. And if you need more money next year, they want you

05:31:53.954 --> 05:31:57.246
to ask for it. So as a result, that's, that's a shift that

05:31:57.746 --> 05:32:01.963
we're trying to make again, which is where we're talking about our taxes basically

05:32:02.076 --> 05:32:05.672
every year to determine what our needs are within the community.

05:32:07.565 --> 05:32:09.637
And that, that is a conversation that,

05:32:11.420 --> 05:32:15.286
that we're not afraid to have when it comes to truth in taxation

05:32:15.786 --> 05:32:19.044
and so forth. That it really is a conversation about what the needs are and

05:32:19.060 --> 05:32:22.780
that we can find that balance and between the

05:32:23.280 --> 05:32:26.568
services that the citizens would like to see and what it would take for

05:32:27.068 --> 05:32:28.156
us to provide that level of service.

05:32:30.428 --> 05:32:33.755
Overall, the goal again with our budget is that it

05:32:33.819 --> 05:32:37.258
is a guide on how we do it. It should be a

05:32:37.274 --> 05:32:40.785
budget as a policy guide, a financial plan, a communication tool,

05:32:40.850 --> 05:32:44.515
and an operations guide. That's always our goals as we

05:32:44.531 --> 05:32:46.950
go into the budget process and what we're looking at.

05:32:49.158 --> 05:32:52.684
And what we will work towards is having

05:32:52.797 --> 05:32:56.951
more of that information up front. Historically, our full

05:32:56.983 --> 05:33:00.340
budget book, it doesn't get released and it's not available until

05:33:00.518 --> 05:33:04.010
after the budget is adopted. And that's where most of the material

05:33:04.510 --> 05:33:08.125
comes to communicate with residents about what's in the

05:33:08.142 --> 05:33:12.098
budget going forward. But it didn't give them that information during the planning

05:33:12.130 --> 05:33:16.005
processes. So those are some of the changes that we will shift to try

05:33:16.505 --> 05:33:20.507
to make sure that really this guiding document can

05:33:20.539 --> 05:33:22.700
function better as a communication tool as well.

05:33:24.880 --> 05:33:27.971
So, as mentioned with the budget process and the timeline,

05:33:28.534 --> 05:33:31.674
we did— this is just kind of highlighting some of the

05:33:32.174 --> 05:33:36.208
things that took place for us in our budget process there. With our kickoff

05:33:36.272 --> 05:33:41.733
that started October. And then the

05:33:41.749 --> 05:33:45.358
budget work meetings that we had as we went in through January, February, all the

05:33:45.858 --> 05:33:49.820
way to the tentative budget in May. And then with our interim budget

05:33:50.030 --> 05:33:54.100
and our Truth in Taxation process. So we

05:33:54.391 --> 05:33:58.168
will be finishing our budget. We have to have everything approved and

05:33:58.200 --> 05:34:00.935
submitted by the end of— by August 31st. 31st,

05:34:01.419 --> 05:34:04.986
and then we have 1 month to get our stuff ready

05:34:05.293 --> 05:34:09.087
to kick off the 2027-28

05:34:09.587 --> 05:34:12.898
budget in October again. So here's

05:34:13.398 --> 05:34:16.424
just a little bit of an overview. This comes from the budget in brief that

05:34:16.521 --> 05:34:20.240
was put out, talking just about some of the

05:34:20.740 --> 05:34:24.578
metrics about our community and the service level delivery

05:34:25.078 --> 05:34:28.575
snapshot that we have about the number of parks, the number of miles

05:34:28.992 --> 05:34:32.422
of roads that we're trying to maintain,

05:34:32.678 --> 05:34:36.750
and the water services and so forth. So just a few of

05:34:36.766 --> 05:34:40.485
those. Obviously, it doesn't cover all of the metrics

05:34:40.985 --> 05:34:43.435
and all that we have going in our community, but just a snapshot.

05:34:44.060 --> 05:34:47.763
And I know earlier in the meeting, there were some additional

05:34:48.263 --> 05:34:52.251
highlights that kind of compared us to some of the other cities too about

05:34:52.751 --> 05:34:57.295
the services that we are providing. Let's see,

05:34:58.090 --> 05:35:01.605
with the fund types, I mentioned this a little bit,

05:35:01.638 --> 05:35:05.423
but I want to talk just a little bit, not to try

05:35:05.923 --> 05:35:09.716
not to be too much, too lengthy, but to really

05:35:10.216 --> 05:35:13.849
talk about the difference between those governmental types of funds versus the business type funds

05:35:14.349 --> 05:35:17.203
and what we have here at Eagle Mountain City.

05:35:18.389 --> 05:35:21.987
So, the ones that have a single asterisk are

05:35:22.487 --> 05:35:26.295
our governmental type funds. So for us, that is the general fund.

05:35:26.890 --> 05:35:29.016
We have what we call special revenue funds.

05:35:30.626 --> 05:35:34.291
And then we have our capital project funds and internal service funds

05:35:34.356 --> 05:35:38.005
and the debt service fund. All of these types of activities

05:35:38.423 --> 05:35:41.687
are really just subaccounts of what we would

05:35:42.187 --> 05:35:45.853
consider our general fund. When we do a special revenue fund,

05:35:45.998 --> 05:35:48.569
it means that they're typically restricted resources,

05:35:50.833 --> 05:35:54.472
like grants, or again, those building and community development

05:35:54.972 --> 05:35:58.015
related fees that are now restricted. We pulled those out into a special revenue fund

05:35:58.515 --> 05:36:01.309
this year. They're funded with charges for services.

05:36:02.146 --> 05:36:05.368
But to have a special revenue fund, you're required to

05:36:05.690 --> 05:36:09.284
be able to generate more revenue from

05:36:09.544 --> 05:36:13.330
basically a charge for service than you would from a transfer in

05:36:14.680 --> 05:36:17.764
to fund those services. So we do have some restrictions.

05:36:18.022 --> 05:36:22.247
And in relation to that, we use special revenue funds for

05:36:22.713 --> 05:36:26.537
some of our impact fee monies because, again, even though we

05:36:27.037 --> 05:36:30.024
have impact fees for water and sewer, they're actually grouped up.

05:36:30.088 --> 05:36:34.555
They're part of the enterprise fund. And so when we're

05:36:34.571 --> 05:36:38.765
looking at the water fund, it is their operations As well,

05:36:38.909 --> 05:36:43.441
and then we will break out and we'll kind of show you the projects that

05:36:43.941 --> 05:36:47.492
are being funded. But those impact fees are classified as

05:36:47.604 --> 05:36:51.316
those enterprise business type activities. So we have a lot of things that kind

05:36:51.816 --> 05:36:56.205
of cross over that can make it a little bit confusing about

05:36:56.302 --> 05:37:00.167
what is being funded when you have what we call a capital projects fund,

05:37:00.248 --> 05:37:03.791
but that doesn't actually include all of the projects for

05:37:03.807 --> 05:37:06.946
the city. It only includes those governmental type projects.

05:37:07.323 --> 05:37:10.689
And then the projects for water and sewer are located

05:37:11.189 --> 05:37:14.969
within those separate functions and stuff.

05:37:17.123 --> 05:37:22.153
So we did have a budget that was proposed that was this $363

05:37:22.170 --> 05:37:25.631
million. There'll be some changes to these numbers that we

