Okay, there are 2 areas

05:38:41.312 --> 05:38:45.472
that are kind of similar that I just wanted to hit on. These are

05:38:45.972 --> 05:38:48.970
also questions that kind of came up that we saw out there in the public.

05:38:49.780 --> 05:38:53.523
One of them is an item that we call cost allocations.

05:38:54.566 --> 05:38:58.949
Sometimes people might know the term as more like an administrative overhead cost.

05:38:59.431 --> 05:39:03.155
I'm going to jump to the next slide and then come back to this

05:39:03.203 --> 05:39:06.575
one. So if you were looking at the online budget

05:39:07.075 --> 05:39:10.267
and you went into one of the funds or one of the service departments,

05:39:10.748 --> 05:39:13.686
you might see a line item that says administrative costs.

05:39:14.682 --> 05:39:18.278
And this one that I'm using as an example here has

05:39:18.310 --> 05:39:21.984
a negative number. And so when you see something like that,

05:39:22.194 --> 05:39:25.367
it's identifying that this is a department that provides

05:39:25.867 --> 05:39:29.872
services to the entire city, but it sits

05:39:30.372 --> 05:39:33.909
within the general fund. And so when we're talking

05:39:34.264 --> 05:39:37.958
cost allocations for us in the general fund, those service

05:39:38.458 --> 05:39:42.320
departments that we have are listed here on this slide. But what we

05:39:42.713 --> 05:39:46.716
do is we're identifying that Even though Human

05:39:46.797 --> 05:39:50.651
Resources sits in the general fund, it provides those

05:39:50.683 --> 05:39:54.021
services for the water and the wastewater,

05:39:55.054 --> 05:39:59.167
stormwater, all the other different fund types that we have that have employees.

05:39:59.232 --> 05:40:01.893
They're also benefiting from that.

05:40:02.603 --> 05:40:03.959
And so the cost allocation,

05:40:05.950 --> 05:40:09.182
we actually have a giant spreadsheet. We use a reciprocal method.

05:40:09.248 --> 05:40:13.252
We have drivers that we create. So on the human resource side,

05:40:13.268 --> 05:40:16.462
we actually use the number of employees that each department has to

05:40:16.962 --> 05:40:20.314
determine their share of the cost that that needs to take place.

05:40:21.116 --> 05:40:24.519
And as a result of that, then you will see an outflow of money.

05:40:24.663 --> 05:40:28.226
So we, we would budget the entire cost for the department,

05:40:28.274 --> 05:40:31.901
but then you'll see an outflow of the administrative costs, which is the share

05:40:31.949 --> 05:40:35.978
of the budget that is being picked up and

05:40:36.058 --> 05:40:39.300
redistributed. to the departments that are

05:40:39.332 --> 05:40:42.140
receiving that benefit. And,

05:40:42.862 --> 05:40:47.050
um, we call those, um, those administrative costs,

05:40:47.275 --> 05:40:49.987
and it— the purpose is so that you can,

05:40:50.661 --> 05:40:54.400
you can see what the full cost is to, to budget the department,

05:40:54.432 --> 05:40:57.770
but you can also see what the full cost would be if we didn't have

05:40:57.802 --> 05:41:00.017
any of those other services involved.

05:41:00.610 --> 05:41:03.643
That, um, like if we didn't have a water or sewer fund and things like

05:41:04.143 --> 05:41:07.432
that, then Part of these costs would go

05:41:07.932 --> 05:41:11.109
away. The benefit is, if you were like, again,

05:41:11.125 --> 05:41:14.851
if you're a special service district and all you do is provide water,

05:41:14.899 --> 05:41:18.463
they have to have all of these same overhead. They need an attorney, they need

05:41:18.963 --> 05:41:22.269
to have a human resource department. The benefit

05:41:22.769 --> 05:41:26.492
to us is that we get to share those costs across

05:41:26.604 --> 05:41:29.735
the organization, very similar to what we do with the sheriff's contract.

05:41:30.891 --> 05:41:34.231
So, because the sheriff's contract is part of the Utah County Sheriff's

05:41:34.731 --> 05:41:38.197
and part of the Utah County administration in general, we get to

05:41:38.245 --> 05:41:41.858
have the benefit of all the administrative services that they provide to

05:41:41.874 --> 05:41:45.165
us, um, that if we

05:41:45.665 --> 05:41:48.666
were doing our own police department, we would then need to pick up, um,

05:41:48.923 --> 05:41:52.680
more additional, um, administrative overhead to be able to provide those

05:41:52.776 --> 05:41:56.493
services as well. Kind of

05:41:56.993 --> 05:42:00.546
similar to that is transfers. So when we're

05:42:01.046 --> 05:42:05.397
talking about transfers, we are required

05:42:05.590 --> 05:42:09.457
to have a separate set of balance sheet accounts and revenue

05:42:09.957 --> 05:42:13.436
and expenditure accounts for every fund that we have, so that we are accounting for

05:42:14.274 --> 05:42:17.725
the money that is generated from those charges or services

05:42:18.144 --> 05:42:20.789
and the type of cost to fund those programs.

05:42:21.766 --> 05:42:27.891
And so when we need to send

05:42:28.391 --> 05:42:31.552
money over to another department, it's done as a transfer.

05:42:32.640 --> 05:42:35.798
Transfers are not a loan. It doesn't create new money for the city,

05:42:35.992 --> 05:42:39.442
but it does inflate our overall budget because you're,

05:42:39.458 --> 05:42:43.110
you're kind of double counting those expenses. You're— it's an

05:42:43.610 --> 05:42:47.205
expense as it goes out of the, of the fund that it's transferring

05:42:47.335 --> 05:42:50.875
out of, and then it's an expense when the money actually is,

05:42:51.613 --> 05:42:53.602
is used for whatever purpose it was.

05:42:54.982 --> 05:42:58.575
In our city, we have

05:42:59.089 --> 05:43:02.586
3 what we call internal service funds,

05:43:03.163 --> 05:43:06.756
meaning the entire purpose for them is to provide services

05:43:06.965 --> 05:43:10.703
to other departments within the city. So for an

05:43:11.203 --> 05:43:14.761
example of that is we have our fleet fund. It's an internal

05:43:15.261 --> 05:43:18.742
service fund. So All of the vehicles that the city owns and all of the

05:43:19.242 --> 05:43:22.948
maintenance costs for those vehicles resides within the fleet

05:43:23.140 --> 05:43:27.009
fund. And every department

05:43:27.074 --> 05:43:30.782
who uses a vehicle has to pay their share towards that.

05:43:31.488 --> 05:43:35.213
And because it's an internal service fund, it happens as a transfer

05:43:35.261 --> 05:43:39.098
rather than a cost allocation. But it functions

05:43:39.114 --> 05:43:42.549
very much the same way. They're— you're paying for your services.

05:43:43.264 --> 05:43:46.384
We do transfers, not just for that type of purpose,

05:43:46.400 --> 05:43:49.809
but the only way to get money to the capital project fund is by

05:43:49.841 --> 05:43:53.772
a transfer. There, there's no

05:43:53.836 --> 05:43:57.196
fees that you generate for those,

05:43:57.389 --> 05:44:01.183
the capital project improvements that we're funding out of that capital project fund.

05:44:01.745 --> 05:44:05.202
So the way that you get money is it is money that you're

05:44:05.411 --> 05:44:08.225
earning in the general fund that then is going to the capital project fund.

05:44:08.400 --> 05:44:12.010
Going over to the capital project fund, or if we get like

05:44:12.787 --> 05:44:16.009
a grant from UDOT or a MAG contribution,

05:44:17.453 --> 05:44:20.837
that is another way that we can have resources. But essentially, it's contribution

05:44:21.337 --> 05:44:24.703
and transfers is the only way that we actually

05:44:24.784 --> 05:44:29.216
get resources into those capital

05:44:29.716 --> 05:44:33.859
improvement projects that are not impact fee eligible or impact fee related.

05:44:36.672 --> 05:44:40.830
This budget year, we do have $94 million in

05:44:41.265 --> 05:44:43.633
capital improvements that are planned.

05:44:45.500 --> 05:44:50.140
We have capital improvements that for

05:44:50.640 --> 05:44:53.632
2026 that won't have been completed.

05:44:53.713 --> 05:44:56.612
